d of Oak Street*. Even DC Head James Gunn has publicly expressed skepticism about AI, aligning with artists' concerns. The growing \"shame\" around AI use in creative fields highlights a significant reckoning within the industry.","shortHeadline":"Supergirl AI Backlash","topic":"AI in Media","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/4369-6a821b7f9746614b490dcc51"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/9QWVmmmB-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"9QWVmmmB","audioDurationS":267,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-17T06:30:49-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-16-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-16","script":"You're listening to The Daily Brief, your essential news update from Forbes. It's Sunday, August 16. Today, we're highlighting the key stories covering US Politics, Geopolitics, and a closer look at US Politics.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nLeading off with, Iran embassy claims karoline leavitt resigned because of decoy plane 'betrayal'—but it's unlikely she was on jet. Sara Dorn has been tracking this development and reports that\n\nAn Iranian embassy in South Africa pushed the social media conspiracy that White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt resigned because President Donald Trump left her on a decoy flight in Turkey to evade an alleged Iranian threat. The embassy on X commended her \"resignation,\" criticizing Trump for \"betrayal.\" The White House, however, denied the rumor, confirming Leavitt was not on the trip. New York Times reporter Maggie Haberman, who covered the original incident, also confirmed Leavitt's absence. Leavitt gave birth May 1, returning to the podium July 16 after being back at work weeks earlier. She announced her resignation Wednesday, citing family reasons and the demanding nature of the Press Secretary role, stating she couldn't be the best mother. Trump expressed understanding, adding she would become a top outside advisor. The \"decoy flight\" involved Trump secretly switching planes via a catering truck in Ankara, Turkey, on July 8, following an alleged Iranian threat, leaving some officials and journalists on the original plane. The threat's legitimacy, however, has been widely disputed.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, Iran tells trump: ‘accept the reality’ he can’t make strait of hormuz U.S. territory, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in Geopolitics.\n\nIran has vehemently rejected former President Donald Trump's assertion that the Strait of Hormuz would become U.S. territory, labeling his comments as \"fanciful delusions.\" Trump declared at a New York rally that he would claim the strategic waterway after defeating Iran. Iranian Deputy Foreign Minister Kazem Gharibabadi stated on X that the Strait \"has been Iranian, is Iranian and will remain Iranian.\" Foreign Ministry spokesperson Esmaeil Baqaei also implicitly criticized Trump, quoting Dostoevsky about self-deception. The Strait of Hormuz remains a critical flashpoint, with both nations claiming control amid ongoing hostilities, including recent Iranian attacks on vessels and U.S. blocking of Iranian ships. The UAE reported an Iranian attack on one of its state-owned oil ships in the strait. Trump also urged Americans to accept higher gas prices, deeming it a necessary cost to prevent Iran from acquiring nuclear weapons. This rhetoric follows a pattern of escalating tensions, with the U.S. maintaining a significant military presence, exemplified by the prolonged deployment of the USS Abraham Lincoln, despite concerns over sailor welfare. Further economic penalties against Iran are also anticipated.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, Trump blasts ‘TREASONISTS’ behind ballroom case in late-night rant after asking for supreme court intervention. This headline is generating significant interest within US Politics.\n\nPresident Donald Trump unleashed a Truth Social tirade late Friday, hours after his administration formally asked the Supreme Court to review the lawsuit blocking his proposed ballroom project. Trump accused the National Trust for Historic Preservation, the plaintiffs, of being \"TREASONISTS\" and revealing \"top secret military features\" of the building in court filings after being shown detailed plans. He also disparaged National Trust board member Alison Hoagland, alleging she has \"absolutely no standing\" and is committing \"gross and flagrant abuse of our Courts.\" The administration's appeal seeks to reverse lower court rulings that demand congressional approval for the project, which Trump views as a national security imperative. The government argues the ballroom, featuring an underground bunker and drone port, is crucial for securely hosting world leaders, even citing an alleged missile threat against Air Force One as justification. Trump, known for initiating controversial renovations, further posted an AI rendering of himself touring the renovated ballroom with George Washington. The administration urged the Supreme Court for an immediate ruling to allow construction, claiming the project is \"moving quickly towards completion.\"\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"Iran Embassy Claims Karoline Leavitt Resigned Because Of Decoy Plane 'Betrayal'—But It's Unlikely She Was On Jet","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/15/iran-embassy-claims-karoline-leavitt-resigned-because-of-decoy-plane-betrayal-but-its-unlikely-she-was-on-jet/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a80c51ab7e2308f04f34c45/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on electi