ald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"President Donald Trump unleashed a Truth Social tirade late Friday, hours after his administration formally asked the Supreme Court to review the lawsuit blocking his proposed ballroom project. Trump accused the National Trust for Historic Preservation, the plaintiffs, of being \"TREASONISTS\" and revealing \"top secret military features\" of the building in court filings after being shown detailed plans. He also disparaged National Trust board member Alison Hoagland, alleging she has \"absolutely no standing\" and is committing \"gross and flagrant abuse of our Courts.\" The administration's appeal seeks to reverse lower court rulings that demand congressional approval for the project, which Trump views as a national security imperative. The government argues the ballroom, featuring an underground bunker and drone port, is crucial for securely hosting world leaders, even citing an alleged missile threat against Air Force One as justification. Trump, known for initiating controversial renovations, further posted an AI rendering of himself touring the renovated ballroom with George Washington. The administration urged the Supreme Court for an immediate ruling to allow construction, claiming the project is \"moving quickly towards completion.\"","shortHeadline":"Trump Legal Battles","topic":"US Politics","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a8087cd9052e2944b70f34b"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/O2vQ1YwP-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"O2vQ1YwP","audioDurationS":289,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-16T08:30:36-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-15-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-15","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, the essential news digest from the Forbes newsroom. It's Saturday, August 15. Today, we're exploring the stories shaping the global conversation—from the latest in Defense and Politics to a deep dive into Crime.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nWe begin with a look at, Trump says USS lincoln hasn’t been deployed ‘nearly long enough’—denies families’ concerns. Sara Dorn has been following this story and reports that\n\nPresident Donald Trump denied concerns from families of USS Abraham Lincoln service members, stating the nine-month deployment wasn't \"nearly long enough,\" drawing immediate lawmaker criticism. Rep. Debbie Dingell and Sen. Andy Kim condemned his \"disregard and disrespect,\" citing direct family outreach. Trump confirmed the ship's imminent replacement, reportedly by the USS George Washington. This follows Sen. Richard Blumenthal's letter detailing \"serious concerns\" about the Lincoln's record 200 consecutive days at sea. Blumenthal cited issues including supply shortages, water contamination, mental health decline, and mail disruptions. Despite this, Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth called concerns \"completely misrepresented.\" Acting Navy Secretary Hung Cao faced emotional family confrontations, with reports of service members expressing suicidal thoughts. The Lincoln was rerouted to the Middle East in January for Iran operations, making only two restricted port calls. Critics warn prolonged deployments, like the USS Gerald R. Ford's 326 days, are a dangerous trend fueled by ongoing conflicts.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou can find the full analysis of that story at Forbes.com. Now, we're turning our attention to, Trump reportedly says his relationship with musk will never be the same after epic fallout, a development that could have a lasting impact on Politics.\n\nPresident Donald Trump and Elon Musk now speak about once a month, following efforts by aides and allies to mend their public feud from last year. While Trump privately acknowledges their relationship won't be the same, artificial intelligence, China, and world events are frequent discussion topics. Key figures like Charlie Kirk, Susie Wiles, and JD Vance worked to rebuild the connection, culminating in a May trip where Musk reportedly joined Trump on Air Force One to Beijing. During this meeting, they discussed Musk's plans for new U.S. factories, family matters, and Musk's intention to spend $100 million to elect Republicans in November. Both men face potential investigations if Democrats regain congressional control. The White House defended Trump's bond with Musk, calling him a \"patriot\" in Trump's \"greatest coalition.\" This reconciliation follows Musk's past criticisms, including his call for Trump's impeachment and accusations regarding Jeffrey Epstein, which he later regretted. Musk recently admitted getting \"a little too involved in politics.\"\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRounding out today's briefing, we look at, Woman charged for allegedly vandalizing world war II memorial, pirro says—after reflecting pool blowback. This story is currently at the center of the conversation in Crime.\n\nU.S. Attorney Jeanine Pirro announced felony charges against Melissa Farris for vandalizing the World War II memorial, calling the spray-painted \"clean hands dirty $\" message and soaped fountain a \"despicable attack.\" Farris's arrest comes as Pirro faces intense criticism from President Trump for dropping a separate high-profile reflecting pool vandalism case. Trump publicly stated Pirro \"folded like an umbrella\" and expressed deep disappointment over the case against Olympian David Hearn. Hearn's lawyers are now urging the judge to prevent prosecutors from reviving charges, citing Trump's remarks as potential \"prosecutorial harassment.\" Federal prosecutors had dropped Hearn's case, acknowledging the reflecting pool damage stemmed from a \"rushed and botched installation,\" not vandalism. Trump later admitted to \"contractor error\" for the July 4th opening, contradicting his initial claims of photographic evidence. However, he still maintained a National Park Service employee witnessed Hearn \"violently\" tugging at the liner, a claim Hearn denies, asserting he only touched a loose sealant piece.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou've reached the end of this Daily Brief. Tune in again for tomorrow's updates. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":0,"articles":[{"title":"Trump Says USS Lincoln Hasn’t Been Deployed ‘Nearly Long Enough’—Denies Families’ Concerns","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/14/trump-says-uss-lincoln-hasnt-been-deployed-nearly-long-enough-denies-families-concerns/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7f606e8d3ecffbd614a36f/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"President Donald Trump denied concerns from families of USS Abraham Lincoln service members, stating the nine-month deployment wasn't \"nearly long enough,\" drawing immediate lawmaker criticism. Rep. Debbie Dingell and Sen. Andy Kim condemned his \"disregard and disrespect,\" citing direct family outreach. Trump confirmed the ship's imminent replacement, reportedly by the USS George Washington. This follows Sen. Richard Blumenthal's letter detailing \"serious concerns\" about the Lincoln's record 200 consecutive days at sea. Blumenthal cited issues including supply shortages, water contamination, mental health decline, and mail disruptions. Despite this, Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth called concerns \"completely misrepresented.\" Acting Navy Secretary Hung Cao faced emotional family confrontations, with reports of service members expressing suicidal thoughts. The Lincoln was rerouted to the Middle East in January for Iran operations, making only two restricted port calls. Critics warn prolonged deployments, like the USS Gerald R. Ford's 326 days, are a dangerous trend fueled by ongoing conflicts.","shortHeadline":"Naval Deployment Debate","topic":"Defense","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a7f603e09671338db968e62"},{"title":"Trump Reportedly Says His Relationship With Musk Will Never Be The Same After Epic Fallout","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/14/trump-reportedly-says-his-relationship-with-musk-will-never-be-the-same-after-epic-fallout/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7f23f0962290bf95d477b4/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":