ction claims.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, Eclipse 2026 headache: what to do if you looked at the sun and your eyes hurt, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in Health.\n\nFollowing the April 8, 2024, total solar eclipse, Google searches for \"eyes hurt\" surged, particularly in areas along the path of totality, indicating many viewers may have lacked proper eye protection. This trend highlights the delayed onset of solar retinopathy symptoms, which can manifest hours or even days after sun exposure. As a partial solar eclipse approaches on August 12, visible across Alaska, 26 U.S. states, and Canada, experts reiterate crucial safety warnings. Viewers must use ISO 12312-2-certified eclipse glasses at all times for any partial eclipse, as ordinary sunglasses offer no protection. Binoculars, cameras, and telescopes require secure solar filters. Solar retinopathy, a painless retinal damage, can cause blurred vision, blind spots, or light sensitivity, and requires immediate medical attention from an ophthalmologist, as there is no proven treatment. While North America experiences a partial eclipse, a total solar eclipse will be visible in eastern Greenland, western Iceland, and northern Spain. Only within this path of totality is it safe to briefly remove eye protection to view the sun's corona. Confusion about this safety rule is common, underscoring the importance of adhering to guidelines to prevent permanent eye damage.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, Why polls got wisconsin governor’s race wrong—after socialist francesca hong loses. This headline is generating significant interest within Politics.\n\nSocialist Francesca Hong, once heavily favored to win the Wisconsin Democratic gubernatorial primary, ultimately lost to Milwaukee County Executive Crowley by a narrow 0.4 percentage points. Betting odds had Hong at 95.5% to win on Monday, though this significantly narrowed to 58% by Tuesday afternoon. Pre-election polls proved unreliable due to several last-minute developments. A Marquette Law poll from late July showed Hong leading with 38%, followed by Lt. Gov. Mandela Barnes at 16% and Crowley at 7%. However, this was taken before Barnes dropped out and shortly after Crowley re-entered the race. Earlier polls also featured high undecided voter percentages and small sample sizes. Hong's controversial 2021 social media posts about canceling Thanksgiving, which gained national attention in early August, further complicated the race dynamics. While a later State Navigate poll showed Hong with 44% to Crowley's 22%, it still underestimated Crowley's eventual support. Crowley secured the endorsement of retiring Governor Tony Evers, while Hong garnered support from some progressive figures like Reps. Ro Khanna and Ilhan Omar, though not from others such as Sen. Bernie Sanders or Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez. The presence of other candidates like Kelda Roys and Joel Brennan, who collectively took over 12% of the vote, also impacted the final outcome.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"MyPillow Founder Mike Lindell Won’t Concede Losing Minnesota Republican Gubernatorial Primary","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/12/mypillow-founder-mike-lindell-wont-concede-losing-minnesota-republican-gubernatorial-primary/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7bfa85542e3bc7a6d30422/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"MyPillow founder Mike Lindell lost the Minnesota GOP gubernatorial primary to House Speaker Lisa Demuth, who secured 43.6% of the vote. Lindell, a prominent promoter of 2020 election fraud claims, finished second with 32.3%. Despite the Associated Press calling the race, Lindell refused to concede, citing unproven \"anomalies.\" His candidacy was notably backed by former President Donald Trump, who praised Lindell's fight for \"free and fair Elections.\" This defeat adds Lindell to a growing list of Trump-endorsed candidates failing in primaries. In the Democratic primary, Senator Amy Klobuchar overwhelmingly won with 89.6%, holding a strong general election lead. Lindell's campaign faced controversies, including residency questions. He also faced a probable campaign law violation for distributing \"Mike Lindell for Governor\" pillows. Furthermore, he recently settled a $1 billion defamation lawsuit with Dominion Voting Systems over his false 2020 election claims.","shortHeadline":"Lindell's Non-Concession","topic":"Politics","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a7bae147fb951a8ac782060"},{"title":"Eclipse 2026 Headache: What To Do If You Looked At The Sun And Your Eyes Hurt","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/jamiecartereurope/2026/08/12/eclipse-2026-headache-what-to-do-if-you-looked-at-the-sun-and-your-eyes-hurt/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a709f99a909ad833a0b79d8/960x0.jpg?cropX1=12\u0026cropX2=4554\u0026cropY1=325\u0026cropY2=2878","author":"Jamie Carter","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3338193","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/jamiecartereurope","authorBio":"Jamie Carter is an award-winning reporter, author, astrotourism guide, eclipse chaser, aurora hunter and stargazer who covers the night sky and space exploration. The world’s foremost solar eclipse journalist and eclipse, Carter edits WhenIsTheNextEclipse.com and works on eclipse and other astrotourism-themed trips for New Scientist Discovery Tours. The co-author of The Eclipse Effect (2026), Complete Guide to the Total Solar Eclipse 2026 (2025) and Complete Guide to the Total Solar Eclipse 2027 (2026), and author of Chasing the Northern Lights (2026), Stargazing In 2026 and A Stargazing Program for Beginners (2015), he received the 2024 Popular Media Award from the American Astronomical Society’s Solar Physics Division for his coverage of the 2024 total solar eclipse. Writing for Forbes.com since 2018, Carter has also reported on the intense geomagnetic storms and global northern lights in 2024-25, incoming comets, the sun’s solar maximum and the search for intelligent life in the cosmos. He also writes for Space.com, Live Science, Sky \u0026 Telescope, BBC Sky At Night and New Scientist.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/60405466b33286b65965b0ce/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"jamiecartereurope","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Following the April 8, 2024, total solar eclipse, Google searches for \"eyes hurt\" surged, particularly in areas along the path of totality, indicating many viewers may have lacked proper eye protection. This trend highlights the delayed onset of solar retinopathy symptoms, which can manifest hours or even days after sun exposure. As a partial solar eclipse approaches on August 12, visible across Alaska, 26 U.S. states, and Canada, experts reiterate crucial safety warnings. Viewers must use ISO 12312-2-certified eclipse glasses at all times for any partial eclipse, as ordinary sunglasses offer no protection. Binoculars, cameras, and telescopes require secure solar filters. Solar retinopathy, a painless retinal damage, can cause blurred vision, blind spots, or light sensitivity, and requires immediate medical attention from an ophthalmologist, as there is no proven treatment. While North America experiences a partial eclipse, a total solar eclipse will be visible in eastern Greenland, western Iceland, and northern Spain. Only within this path of totality is it safe to briefly remove eye protection to view the sun's corona. Confusion about this safety rule is common, underscoring the importance of adhering to guidelines to prevent permanent eye damage.","shortHeadline":"Eclipse Eye Damage","topic":"Health","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/6117-6a709ef8b905055dc9881382"},{"title":"Why Polls Got Wisconsin Governor’s Race Wrong—After Socialist Francesca Hong Loses","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/12/why-polls-got-wisconsin-governors-race-wrong-after-socialist-francesca-hong-loses/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7c77844010efdc5a65bd04/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Socialist Francesca Hong, once heavily favored to win the Wisconsin Democratic gubernatorial primary, ultimately lost to Milwaukee County Executive Crowley by a narrow 0.4 percentage points. Betting odds had Hong at 95.5% to win on Monday, though this significantly narrowed to 58% by Tuesday afternoon. Pre-election polls proved unreliable due to several last-minute developments. A Marquette Law poll from late July showed Hong leading with 38%, followed by Lt. Gov. Mandela Barnes at 16% and Crowley at 7%. However, this was taken before Barnes dropped out and shortly after Crowley re-entered the race. Earlier polls also featured high undecided voter percentages and small sample sizes. Hong's controversial 2021 social media posts about canceling Thanksgiving, which gained national attention in early August, further complicated the race dynamics. While a later State Navigate poll showed Hong with 44% to Crowley's 22%, it still underestimated Crowley's eventual support. Crowley secured the endorsement of retiring Governor Tony Evers, while Hong garnered support from some progressive figures like Reps. Ro Khanna and Ilhan Omar, though not from others such as Sen. Bernie Sanders or Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez. The presence of other candidates like Kelda Roys and Joel Brennan, who collectively took over 12% of the vote, also impacted the final outcome.","shortHeadline":"Polling Inaccuracies","topic":"Politics","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a7c7746d0b1f0727f25c046"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/TgtN8KcZ-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"TgtN8KcZ","audioDurationS":296,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-13T06:30:35-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-12-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-12","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, the essential news digest from the Forbes newsroom. It's Wednesday, August 12. Today, we're exploring the stories shaping the global conversation—from the latest in Politics and Tourism to a deep dive into Ecotourism.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nWe begin with a look at, How left-wing twitch streamer hasan piker has divided democrats. Sara Dorn has been following this story and reports that\n\nLeft-wing Twitch streamer Hasan Piker has become a central figure in the Democratic Party's internal struggle over the progressive movement, with his incendiary politics drawing fire from both conservatives and centrist Democrats. A recent Rolling Stone profile reignited debate, prompting conservatives like Alyssa Farrah Griffin and Elon Musk to criticize Piker and, by extension, the Democratic Party. Centrist Democrats, including Joe Scarborough, have urged progressives to distance themselves, citing Piker's controversial anti-Israel rhetoric and past comments, such as his 2019 statement that \"America deserved 9/11.\" Michigan Democratic Senate nominee Abdul El-Sayed faced intense backlash for campaigning with Piker, with figures like Sen. John Fetterman and strategist James Carville condemning the association. While El-Sayed partially distanced himself from Piker's \"dumb statement\" on 9/11, he defended appearing with him, arguing that association doesn't imply full agreement. Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, who along with other progressives like Rashida Tlaib and Ilhan Omar has appeared with Piker, emphasized the need for Democrats to engage young voters online. Piker, known for his staunch anti-Israel views and often hyperbolic rhetoric, has characterized some of his controversial statements as performance. The ongoing debate highlights concerns within the Democratic Party about the electability of progressive candidates, many of whom Piker has supported, in upcoming general elections.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou can find the full analysis of that story at Forbes.com. Now, we're turning our attention to, Canadian visitors to U.S. were up 10% in july—but new steep tariffs threaten tourism lift, a development that could have a lasting impact on Tourism.\n\nCanadian visitor numbers to the U.S. rose 10% in July compared to last year, marking the fourth consecutive month of improvement. This increase, primarily driven by a 13% rise in car trips, is largely attributed to a \"base-year effect\" following exceptionally low visitation in 2025. Despite this uptick, overall Canadian travel to the U.S. remains down 29% compared to July 2024, with air travel also slightly down. Tourism leaders, however, caution that the Trump administration's new round of 50% punitive tariffs, set to take effect on August 19, could reverse this nascent recovery.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRounding out today's briefing, we look at, Wolf populations are soaring across europe, and so is wildlife tourism. This story is currently at the center of the conversation in Ecotourism.\n\nWolves are making an astonishing comeback across Europe, with populations surging by 58% to over 21,500 individuals by 2022, a testament to decades of legal protection and ecological recovery. This remarkable return, often in areas previously devoid of large carnivores, has transformed the wolf from a historical adversary into a symbol of rewilding and a magnet for ecotourism. Specialized outfitters now offer unique experiences, from tracking packs in Italy and Sweden to overnight photography in Finnish hides, catering to a growing desire to connect with wild nature. These responsible tours, emphasizing the thrill of potential sightings over guarantees, are crucial for local economies. They inject vital revenue into rural communities, supporting guides and accommodations, and fostering a \"virtuous cycle\" where wildlife presence drives economic growth, as seen in Italy's Abruzzo region. While recent policy changes may allow for more flexible wolf management, the species' resurgence highlights its resilience and its new role in supporting sustainable green economies across the continent.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou've reached the end of this Daily Brief. Tune in again for tomorrow's updates. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":0,"articles":[{"title":"How Left-Wing Twitch Streamer Hasan Piker Has Divided Democrats","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/11/how-left-wing-twitch-streamer-hasan-piker-has-divided-democrats/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7b2fdb6716f540e84c15ac/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Left-wing Twitch streamer Hasan Piker has become a central figure in the Democratic Party's internal struggle over the progressive movement, with his incendiary politics drawing fire from both conservatives and centrist Democrats. A recent Rolling Stone profile reignited debate, prompting conservatives like Alyssa Farrah Griffin and Elon Musk to criticize Piker and, by extension, the Democratic Party. Centrist Democrats, including Joe Scarborough, have urged progressives to distance themselves, citing Piker's controversial anti-Israel rhetoric and past comments, such as his 2019 statement that \"America deserved 9/11.\" Michigan Democratic Senate nominee Abdul El-Sayed faced intense backlash for campaigning with Piker, with figures like Sen. John Fetterman and strategist James Carville condemning the association. While El-Sayed partially distanced himself from Piker's \"dumb statement\" on 9/11, he defended appearing with him, arguing that association doesn't imply full agreement. Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, who along with other progressives like Rashida Tlaib and Ilhan Omar has appeared with Piker, emphasized the need for Democrats to engage young voters online. Piker, known for his staunch anti-Israel views and often hyperbolic rhetoric, has characterized some of his controversial statements as performance. The ongoing debate highlights concerns within the Democratic Party about the electability of progressive candidates, many of whom Piker has supported, in upcoming general elections.","shortHeadline":"Democratic Divisions","topic":"Politics","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a7b2fbb3e41891fb1be117a"},{"title":"Canadian Visitors To U.S. Were Up 10% In July—But New Steep Tariffs Threaten Tourism Lift","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/2026/08/11/canadian-visitors-july-tariffs-threaten-tourism/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7b2ddf660cfbc141c571f8/960x0.jpg","author":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3340630","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/","authorBio":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher covers travel for Forbes. Follow her on Bluesky @srkelleher.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/601b7235b35a3ce5939c28fe/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"suzannerowankelleher","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Canadian visitor numbers to the U.S. rose 10% in July compared to last year, marking the fourth consecutive month of improvement. This increase, primarily driven by a 13% rise in car trips, is largely attributed to a \"base-year effect\" following exceptionally low visitation in 2025. Despite this uptick, overall Canadian travel to the U.S. remains down 29% compared to July 2024, with air travel also slightly down. Tourism leaders, however, caution that the Trump administration's new round of 50% punitive tariffs, set to take effect on August 19, could reverse this nascent recovery.","shortHeadline":"Tourism Tariffs","topic":"Tourism","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/7082-6a7b2d9243ec45a7bb3fef00"},{"title":"Wolf Populations Are Soaring Across Europe, And So Is Wildlife Tourism","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/jimdobson/2026/08/11/wolf-populations-are-soaring-across-europe-and-so-is-wildlife-tourism/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a6f389794742b46f4015d51/960x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1637\u0026cropY1=6\u0026cropY2=927","author":"Jim Dobson","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/1302351","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/jimdobson/","authorBio":"Jim Dobson is an award-winning writer, author, and professional raconteur, specializing in exclusive travel, extreme luxury, and high-end destinations.For over 12 years, he has been writing for Forbes, focusing on rare access to extraordinary people, places, and things such as superyachts, underground bunkers, and luxury real estate.An American based in Italy, Dobson has spent over 45 years traveling and has visited more than 90 countries. His work emphasizes immersive, firsthand experiences in remote destinations, often highlighting places that are largely inaccessible to the public.Before focusing on journalism, Dobson built a career in the entertainment industry, working with prominent celebrities and billionaires. This experience informs his access-driven approach and narrative style.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a71fcd9e81e7a6944059bdc/400x0.png?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=800\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=800","authorSlug":"jimdobson","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Wolves are making an astonishing comeback across Europe, with populations surging by 58% to over 21,500 individuals by 2022, a testament to decades of legal protection and ecological recovery. This remarkable return, often in areas previously devoid of large carnivores, has transformed the wolf from a historical adversary into a symbol of rewilding and a magnet for ecotourism. Specialized outfitters now offer unique experiences, from tracking packs in Italy and Sweden to overnight photography in Finnish hides, catering to a growing desire to connect with wild nature. These responsible tours, emphasizing the thrill of potential sightings over guarantees, are crucial for local economies. They inject vital revenue into rural communities, supporting guides and accommodations, and fostering a \"virtuous cycle\" where wildlife presence drives economic growth, as seen in Italy's Abruzzo region. While recent policy changes may allow for more flexible wolf management, the species' resurgence highlights its resilience and its new role in supporting sustainable green economies across the continent.","shortHeadline":"Wildlife Tourism Boom","topic":"Ecotourism","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/3307-6a6f357d36f53e82b37ee2a1"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/gKLRq7U7-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"gKLRq7U7","audioDurationS":272,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-12T06:30:43-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-11-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-11","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Tuesday, August 11. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Corporate Earnings, Political Strategy, and Political Candidates.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Corporate Earnings space, we start with, Geico earnings plummet 45% in hit to berkshire hathaway's insurance business. According to reporting from Mary Whitfill Roeloffs\n\nBerkshire Hathaway's Geico reported a nearly 45% drop in Q2 2026 pre-tax underwriting earnings, falling to $994 million. This significant decline, impacting its most profitable sector, stemmed from increased auto claims and sharply rising bodily injury costs. Geico's loss ratio climbed to 76.6% in Q2 and 75.3% for the first half, up nearly five percentage points. Bodily injury claims rose 5% in frequency and 10% in average cost during H1 2026, worsening from 2025 and reflecting a trend where injury payouts now exceed physical damage claims. The surge stems from social factors like higher medical costs, affordability issues, and aggressive legal strategies, not accident frequency; attorney involvement and rising healthcare expenses inflate costs. ADAS has also reduced minor collisions, shifting the claims pool disproportionately towards severe, injury-prone crashes. This operational deterioration is notable given Geico's recent recovery, despite Berkshire Hathaway's robust financial cushion.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, Why republicans might be rooting for socialist francesca hong in wisconsin primary, a story that could have significant implications for Political Strategy.\n\nDemocratic socialist State Rep. Francesca Hong leads Wisconsin's gubernatorial primary polls, raising alarms among some Democrats about her general election viability against a Republican. This concern reflects a national trend where progressive primary winners face electability doubts. Surveys show Hong with a significant lead, though many voters remain undecided. Incumbent Gov. Tony Evers, who endorsed opponent David Crowley, openly questioned Hong's ability to defeat presumptive Republican nominee Tom Tiffany and wouldn't commit to supporting her. Crowley also stated, \"people don't like socialists,\" believing Hong \"can't win.\" Polling suggests Hong could lose support among primary voters to Tiffany in November, with an earlier Marquette poll showing her trailing him. Republicans, viewing Hong as a weaker candidate, are already running ads through groups like Right Direction Wisconsin, branding her too liberal and highlighting her call to abolish ICE. This strategy is seen by some as an attempt to boost Hong's primary chances, aiming for a more beatable general election opponent.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, Darline graham’s riding with trump—but political views beyond that still unclear as primary looms. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Political Candidates.\n\nSen. Darline Graham Nordone, R-S.C., the younger sister of the late Sen. Lindsey Graham, announced her bid to permanently succeed her brother, whom she was appointed to replace temporarily. Nordone, who served as commissioner for the South Carolina Commission for the Blind since 2019, has a background in disability rights advocacy, including helping to end subminimum wage policy in the state. Colleagues describe her as not overtly political, with her only recorded political donation being $100. She was raised by her brother after their parents' deaths when she was 13 and often campaigned with him. Nordone expressed confidence in her ability to serve, stating she learned hard work from Lindsey. Her candidacy comes with an endorsement from former President Donald Trump, who called her a \"spectacular person.\" She will compete in a special primary on August 11 against Republican Reps. Russell Fry and Ralph Norman, among others, with the winner facing Democrat Annie Andrews in November. Lindsey Graham, a four-term senator and foreign policy hawk, died on July 11.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"Geico Earnings Plummet 45% In Hit To Berkshire Hathaway's Insurance Business","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs/2026/08/10/geico-earnings-plummet-45-in-hit-to-berkshire-hathaways-insurance-business/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a79ebfcc7e46bf932eaae55/960x0.jpg","author":"Mary Whitfill Roeloffs","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/7236790","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/maryroeloffs","authorBio":"Mary Roeloffs is a Forbes reporter who covers breaking news with a frequent focus on the entertainment industry, streaming, pop culture, politics, public health and climate change. She joined Forbes in 2023 and lives in Rochester, N.Y. She’s covered Hollywood’s most contentious lawsuits, allegations against Sean “Diddy” Combs, the Trump administration’s controversies, how AI is changing the job market and the 2026 hantavirus outbreak. Roeloffs was included on Editor \u0026 Publisher Magazine’s “25 Under 30” list in 2023 and worked covering local news in the greater Boston area from 2017 to 2023. She graduated with a double major in political science and journalism from Northeastern University. Follow Roeloffs for continued coverage of streaming wars, pop culture news and trending topics.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6487561380bb7d9077f4b945/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=400\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=400","authorSlug":"maryroeloffs","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Berkshire Hathaway's Geico reported a nearly 45% drop in Q2 2026 pre-tax underwriting earnings, falling to $994 million. This significant decline, impacting its most profitable sector, stemmed from increased auto claims and sharply rising bodily injury costs. Geico's loss ratio climbed to 76.6% in Q2 and 75.3% for the first half, up nearly five percentage points. Bodily injury claims rose 5% in frequency and 10% in average cost during H1 2026, worsening from 2025 and reflecting a trend where injury payouts now exceed physical damage claims. The surge stems from social factors like higher medical costs, affordability issues, and aggressive legal strategies, not accident frequency; attorney involvement and rising healthcare expenses inflate costs. ADAS has also reduced minor collisions, shifting the claims pool disproportionately towards severe, injury-prone crashes. This operational deterioration is notable given Geico's recent recovery, despite Berkshire Hathaway's robust financial cushion.","shortHeadline":"Insurance Business Woes","topic":"Corporate Earnings","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51951-6a79eb40e6e04d246ecbda91"},{"title":"Why Republicans Might Be Rooting For Socialist Francesca Hong In Wisconsin Primary","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/10/why-republicans-might-be-rooting-for-socialist-francesca-hong-in-wisconsin-primary/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a79f074f946de665d0a6e25/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Democratic socialist State Rep. Francesca Hong leads Wisconsin's gubernatorial primary polls, raising alarms among some Democrats about her general election viability against a Republican. This concern reflects a national trend where progressive primary winners face electability doubts. Surveys show Hong with a significant lead, though many voters remain undecided. Incumbent Gov. Tony Evers, who endorsed opponent David Crowley, openly questioned Hong's ability to defeat presumptive Republican nominee Tom Tiffany and wouldn't commit to supporting her. Crowley also stated, \"people don't like socialists,\" believing Hong \"can't win.\" Polling suggests Hong could lose support among primary voters to Tiffany in November, with an earlier Marquette poll showing her trailing him. Republicans, viewing Hong as a weaker candidate, are already running ads through groups like Right Direction Wisconsin, branding her too liberal and highlighting her call to abolish ICE. This strategy is seen by some as an attempt to boost Hong's primary chances, aiming for a more beatable general election opponent.","shortHeadline":"Primary Election Dynamics","topic":"Political Strategy","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a79f03e008547865a71c6c3"},{"title":"Darline Graham’s Riding With Trump—But Political Views Beyond That Still Unclear As Primary Looms","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/10/darline-grahams-riding-with-trump-but-political-views-beyond-that-still-unclear-as-primary-looms/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a5e9e3e7c769d99ff3add62/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Sen. Darline Graham Nordone, R-S.C., the younger sister of the late Sen. Lindsey Graham, announced her bid to permanently succeed her brother, whom she was appointed to replace temporarily. Nordone, who served as commissioner for the South Carolina Commission for the Blind since 2019, has a background in disability rights advocacy, including helping to end subminimum wage policy in the state. Colleagues describe her as not overtly political, with her only recorded political donation being $100. She was raised by her brother after their parents' deaths when she was 13 and often campaigned with him. Nordone expressed confidence in her ability to serve, stating she learned hard work from Lindsey. Her candidacy comes with an endorsement from former President Donald Trump, who called her a \"spectacular person.\" She will compete in a special primary on August 11 against Republican Reps. Russell Fry and Ralph Norman, among others, with the winner facing Democrat Annie Andrews in November. Lindsey Graham, a four-term senator and foreign policy hawk, died on July 11.","shortHeadline":"Candidate's Political Views","topic":"Political Candidates","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a5e9e0dd52c2c3659456539"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/LEeOtn8T-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"LEeOtn8T","audioDurationS":273,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-11T06:30:44-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-10-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-10","script":"You're listening to The Daily Brief, your essential news update from Forbes. It's Monday, August 10. Today, we're highlighting the key stories covering Dining, Public Health, and a closer look at Entrepreneurship.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nLeading off with, 7 new london restaurants to try, summer 2026. Joanne Shurvell has been tracking this development and reports that\n\nLondon's summer 2026 culinary landscape is vibrant with ambitious new openings, spanning diverse international cuisines. Mayfair's Mimi Mei Fair offers contemporary Thai, serving spicy classics and fire-led dishes like Peking Duck Penang Curry in a Georgian setting. In Soho, Miokuru provides an intimate 20-seat chef's counter, refining British seafood with Japanese techniques, featuring exquisite crab hand rolls and seared trout sashimi. The OWO has welcomed Langosteria, Milan's renowned seafood institution, bringing glamorous Italian dining with expertly grilled fish and signature King Crab. Borough's Oudh 1722, from Michelin-starred Aktar Islam, introduces royal Awadhi cuisine, featuring street-food snacks, kebabs, and slow-cooked dishes like oxtail biryani. MA/NA, in Mayfair, presents a Japanese menu with elegant sushi, robata-grilled items, and inventive small plates. Cafe Clement offers stylish all-day French and Italian dining, led by former River Café chef Danny Bohan. Latine, also in Mayfair, blends classic French techniques with vibrant Latin American flavors, featuring Boeuf Bourguignon Tacos, a dedicated Crudo Bar, and unique desserts.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, Microplastics are part of everyday life. what does that mean for our health?, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in Public Health.\n\nPlastic is everywhere: in our buildings, clothing, food, cleaning and personal care products, and countless things we use every day. Scientists are now finding microscopic plastic particles throughout the human body, including the brain. What that means for our health is a much bigger question, and one researchers are only beginning to answer.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, How azature pogosian bet on anti-scale strategy and won. This headline is generating significant interest within Entrepreneurship.\n\nThe global luxury jewelry market is rapidly expanding, projected to exceed $580 billion by decade's end, increasingly leveraging digital strategies and accessibility. Yet, Los Angeles jeweler Azature Pogosian, dubbed the \"Black Diamond King,\" built his brand on a contrasting philosophy. For nearly 25 years, he has prioritized deep relationships, bespoke craftsmanship, and organic word-of-mouth over mass reach and extensive ad spend. His accidental journey began with a $5,000 investment from his uncle, steering him from fashion into jewelry. Pogosian rose to prominence by transforming overlooked black diamonds into highly coveted pieces, notably gaining traction when Rihanna wore his designs. Pogosian defines true luxury by craftsmanship, the time invested, and the personal meaning of an \"eternal heirloom,\" not merely its cost. He consistently rejects mass distribution, asserting fine jewelry is too personal for off-the-shelf sales. This distinctive approach allows him to thrive amidst a diamond market increasingly shaped by lab-grown stones, emphasizing lasting value and legacy.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"7 New London Restaurants To Try, Summer 2026","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/joanneshurvell/2026/08/09/7-new-london-restaurants-to-try-summer-2026/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a70a1ae05d35267f61d7a8b/960x0.jpg","author":"Joanne Shurvell","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/1986577","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/joanneshurvell/","authorBio":"London-based writer Joanne Shurvell covers travel, food, culture and fashion for a variety of publications, including Forbes since 2016. She co-founded PayneShurvell, a contemporary art gallery in London and was co-author of the YouTube art series, Great Art Cities Explained with James Payne. She is co-author of the Citysketch series of books that includes London, Paris and New York and the author of Fantastic Forgeries: Paint Like Van Gogh. Her photographer partner Paul Allen often supplies photos for her features. Follow them on Instagram at @joshurvell and @andfotography2.","authorAvatar":"h