urnalist with nearly a decade of experience chronicling the stories behind successful entrepreneurs and innovative brands. Her coverage spans a diverse range of lifestyle topics, with a particular focus on beauty, fashion, travel, and wellbeing. Harris is known for her willingness to share her personal experiences. She has openly written about her journey of treating depression and anxiety with clinician-guided ketamine therapy—a pioneering approach to treating mental health challenges with psychedelics. She has also provided candid accounts of her adventures exploring the latest non-invasive aesthetic treatments and her experiences at some of the world's most notable longevity clinics. Harris's impressive roster of interviewees includes renowned CEOs, celebrity-entrepreneurs, trailblazing startup founders, and medical leaders shaping the future of longevity medicine, aesthetics, biohacking, and mental health. Follow Harris for her incisive perspective on the dynamic, rapidly evolving spaces of entrepreneurship, wellness, and lifestyle innovation.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6400d72ac69492121c55cb52/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=970\u0026cropY1=3\u0026cropY2=973","authorSlug":"meggenharris","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Plastic is everywhere: in our buildings, clothing, food, cleaning and personal care products, and countless things we use every day. Scientists are now finding microscopic plastic particles throughout the human body, including the brain. What that means for our health is a much bigger question, and one researchers are only beginning to answer.","shortHeadline":"Microplastic Health Risks","topic":"Public Health","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/4251-6a790669008547865a71c6ab"},{"title":"How Azature Pogosian Bet On Anti-Scale Strategy And Won","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/stephanrabimov/2026/08/09/how-azature-pogosian-bet-on-anti-scale-strategy-and-won/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a79454ee35302ff50c30ffe/960x0.jpg","author":"Stephan Rabimov","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/2047632","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/stephanrabimov/","authorBio":"I’ve worked as a journalist, in digital and print publishing, public relations and marketing for over a decade. I have written and reported extensively about fashion and emerging markets, as well as contemporary culture, lifestyle, diplomacy, education, and the arts. I am a founder and editor-in-chief of DEPESHA magazine, and successfully launched Thom Browne, Rubin Singer, and Custo Barcelona brands in Russia, and Nicola Formichetti’s pop-up store in NYC. I have been interviewed and published across dozens of publications, including The New York Times Russia Beyond The Headlines Edition, Forbes International, The Huffington Post, Women's Wear Daily, L'Officiel, Vogue, Wall Street Journal, CNN, Yahoo Style, and Examiner. For Forbes.com I write about emerging fashion markets. Email me stephan.rabimov@gmail.com to be a part of my world.","authorAvatar":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f336d56304b935f5ac961c9b52ccb88a?s=400\u0026d=mm\u0026r=g","authorSlug":"stephanrabimov","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"The global luxury jewelry market is rapidly expanding, projected to exceed $580 billion by decade's end, increasingly leveraging digital strategies and accessibility. Yet, Los Angeles jeweler Azature Pogosian, dubbed the \"Black Diamond King,\" built his brand on a contrasting philosophy. For nearly 25 years, he has prioritized deep relationships, bespoke craftsmanship, and organic word-of-mouth over mass reach and extensive ad spend. His accidental journey began with a $5,000 investment from his uncle, steering him from fashion into jewelry. Pogosian rose to prominence by transforming overlooked black diamonds into highly coveted pieces, notably gaining traction when Rihanna wore his designs. Pogosian defines true luxury by craftsmanship, the time invested, and the personal meaning of an \"eternal heirloom,\" not merely its cost. He consistently rejects mass distribution, asserting fine jewelry is too personal for off-the-shelf sales. This distinctive approach allows him to thrive amidst a diamond market increasingly shaped by lab-grown stones, emphasizing lasting value and legacy.","shortHeadline":"Entrepreneurial Success","topic":"Entrepreneurship","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/4398-6a793fb63358f564a50b8e23"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/FpeLxCUg-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"FpeLxCUg","audioDurationS":232,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-10T06:30:40-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-09-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-09","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, the essential news digest from the Forbes newsroom. It's Sunday, August 9. Today, we're exploring the stories shaping the global conversation—from the latest in Sports Policy and Geopolitics to a deep dive into Entertainment.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nWe begin with a look at, Ex-NBA players say they’re ‘declaring’ for WNBA as league considers transgender player policy. Mary Whitfill Roeloffs has been following this story and reports that\n\nFormer NBA players Royce White and Enes Kanter Freedom have provocatively declared their intent to enter the 2027 WNBA Draft, challenging the league's eligibility standards for transgender athletes. Kanter, who legally changed his name to include \"Freedom,\" stated he reviewed the WNBA's framework and believes he qualifies under \"self-identification and inclusion\" rules. White, who played briefly in the NBA, echoed this, telling Fox News he \"identifies as sometimes identifying as a woman\" for basketball purposes, claiming he'd be \"unstoppable.\" Both assert their actions are not to mock, but to push the WNBA to clarify its guidelines on who can play. This move intensifies a debate sparked by Indiana Fever star Sophie Cunningham, who opposed transgender women in the league, and further fueled by OutKick's interaction with French transgender player Julie Tétart. The WNBA's Collective Bargaining Agreement states \"Only players who are women are eligible,\" but lacks a definition for \"woman\" or a clause for eligibility based solely on self-identification. WNBA Commissioner Cathy Engelbert announced a task force to discuss the issue, emphasizing preserving game integrity. The WNBA Players Association, meanwhile, stressed diversity and inclusion, rejecting being used as \"political pawns\" and condemning hate against transgender people. Kanter summarized his position, stating, \"If simply declaring who you are is all that’s required, then I meet every single requirement necessary to compete in the WNBA.\"\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou can find the full analysis of that story at Forbes.com. Now, we're turning our attention to, Iran says U.S. must meet demands before strait of hormuz reopens, a development that could have a lasting impact on Geopolitics.\n\nIran has presented a comprehensive list of demands to the United States, declaring it will not reopen the vital Strait of Hormuz to marine traffic until these conditions are met. Mohammad Bagher Zolghadr, head of Iran’s Supreme National Security Council, outlined these requirements, which were published by Iranian media on Saturday. Key demands include the lifting of the U.S. naval blockade, an end to all sanctions, and the withdrawal of American troops from the region. Iran also insists the U.S. cease threatening the country, halt strikes in Iran, Lebanon, Palestine, Yemen, and Iraq permanently, withdraw all naval and air forces, pay war reparations, and release frozen Iranian assets. These stringent conditions significantly complicate ongoing talks between Iran and Oman, which were previously expected to lead to the waterway's reopening and a subsequent relaxation of global energy prices. Iran's foreign minister, Abbas Araghchi, confirmed that while an agreement with Oman was \"very close,\" the Strait's reopening remains contingent on the U.S. fulfilling these demands. However, American officials have consistently stated that sanctions relief is tied to a nuclear deal, and the release of frozen assets requires Iran to abandon its highly enriched uranium program. The Trump administration had not publicly commented on Iran's latest demands as of Saturday afternoon, with observers noting the U.S. is highly unlikely to agree to such extensive concessions.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRounding out today's briefing, we look at, Where is macy gray now? the ‘I try’ singer is still full of surprises. This story is currently at the center of the conversation in Entertainment.\n\nMacy Gray, iconic for her 1999 hit \"I Try\" and distinctive raspy voice, recently delivered a confident and joyful performance at the Blue Note in Los Angeles. Her path to stardom was challenging; initially teased for her voice, she entered music accidentally after studying screenwriting. An early record deal with Atlantic failed, coinciding with her marriage's end, prompting her return to Ohio with her children, nearly abandoning music. However, executive Jeff Blue rediscovered her unreleased tracks, signing her under the alias \"Mushroom\" to secure a new deal with Epic Records in 1998. Her debut album, *On How Life Is*, eventually launched her to international fame with \"I Try.\" Now 58, Gray's voice has deepened, reflecting a seasoned artist who has shed her earlier shyness. Beyond music, she has acted in films like *Training Day*, covered Stevie Wonder's *Talking Book*, and appeared on *The Masked Singer*. Gray is scheduled to tour Australia, New Zealand, and Europe in 2026, with a new album, \"The Trouble With Truth,\" expected this fall, showcasing her enduring artistic vitality.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou've reached the end of this Daily Brief. Tune in again for tomorrow's updates. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId"