probe he had requested himself to clear his name amidst the allegations.","shortHeadline":"Campaign Disputes","topic":"Political Campaigns","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a722c09cbe9922b9fc4b468"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/JNbjrbSW-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"JNbjrbSW","audioDurationS":275,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-07T06:30:38-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-06-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-06","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, the essential news digest from the Forbes newsroom. It's Thursday, August 6. Today, we're exploring the stories shaping the global conversation—from the latest in US Elections and Interior Design to a deep dive into Fine Spirits.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nWe begin with a look at, Establishment democrats line up behind progressive el-sayed after tight race divided party. Sara Dorn has been following this story and reports that\n\nAbdul El-Sayed narrowly clinched Michigan's Democratic Senate nomination, quickly uniting centrist Democrats, including primary opponent Rep. Haley Stevens, Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer, and Gov. Gretchen Whitmer. They pledged support against Republican Mike Rogers, aiming to check Donald Trump and flip the Senate. Still, some party division emerged, with Sen. John Fetterman downplaying El-Sayed's slim victory. President Trump and allies immediately attacked El-Sayed's progressive views, labeling him a \"Communist loser\" and \"radical anti-Israel\" to push their narrative of a far-left Democratic Party and boost Rogers, whom Trump called an easier opponent. El-Sayed, a progressive endorsed by figures like Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, defended his commitment to Jewish safety while maintaining his stance on U.S. aid to Israel and Gaza, rejecting antisemitism claims. Pro-Israel groups, previously backing Stevens, indicated they would now support Rogers. El-Sayed's tight win, despite earlier double-digit poll leads, signifies another high-profile progressive primary success this cycle.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou can find the full analysis of that story at Forbes.com. Now, we're turning our attention to, Valspar kicks off 2027 color of the year season with ‘cottage door’, a development that could have a lasting impact on Interior Design.\n\nValspar Paints has launched the 2027 Color of the Year season, naming Cottage Door (8004-38E) as its selection. This mid-tone neutral blue is described as seductively serene, offering calm and an antidote to constant stimulation. Valspar's director, Sue Kim, noted its all-over neutrality, even using it in her bedroom for a cozy, anchored feel. Blue hues are a strong trend, appearing in 2026 reports from Houzz and WGSN/Coloro, which picked \"Luminous Blue\" for 2027. Major brands like Glidden, Sherwin-Williams, and Benjamin Moore have also recently chosen blue shades. Valspar itself previously selected Encore (2025) and Renew Blue, prior to its 2026 choice of Warm Eucalyptus.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRounding out today's briefing, we look at, Top islay and campbeltown scotch whisky, from the global spirits masters. This story is currently at the center of the conversation in Fine Spirits.\n\nCampbeltown and Islay, despite their modest whisky output, consistently produce some of Scotland's most highly regarded single malts, a reputation strongly reinforced at the 2025 Global Spirits Masters. Campbeltown's Glen Scotia impressed across its core range and limited editions, earning a prestigious Tastemaster Award for its 2026 Malts Festival Edition, alongside Master Medals for its Harbor, 12-Year-Old, and 15-Year-Old expressions, all showcasing elegant maritime character. Islay's Kilchoman and the iconic Lagavulin also secured Master Medals, demonstrating the enduring appeal of expertly balanced peat smoke, coastal influence, and long maturation. Kilchoman's Rockside 11 Years Old highlighted its unique farm-to-bottle style, while Lagavulin 16-Year-Old exemplified its rich peat smoke and maritime elegance. These award-winning whiskies underscore the individuality and world-class quality defining Scotland's western whisky regions. Glen Scotia's performance confirms its growing reputation for refined maritime malts, while Kilchoman and Lagavulin solidify Islay's status as the home of Scotland's most expressive peated whiskies, showcasing diverse and characterful styles.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou've reached the end of this Daily Brief. Tune in again for tomorrow's updates. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":0,"articles":[{"title":"Establishment Democrats Line Up Behind Progressive El-Sayed After Tight Race Divided Party","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/05/establishment-democrats-line-up-behind-progressive-el-sayed-after-tight-race-divided-party/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a7398d78c976550e2cf97e9/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Abdul El-Sayed narrowly clinched Michigan's Democratic Senate nomination, quickly uniting centrist Democrats, including primary opponent Rep. Haley Stevens, Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer, and Gov. Gretchen Whitmer. They pledged support against Republican Mike Rogers, aiming to check Donald Trump and flip the Senate. Still, some party division emerged, with Sen. John Fetterman downplaying El-Sayed's slim victory. President Trump and allies immediately attacked El-Sayed's progressive views, labeling him a \"Communist loser\" and \"radical anti-Israel\" to push their narrative of a far-left Democratic Party and boost Rogers, whom Trump called an easier opponent. El-Sayed, a progressive endorsed by figures like Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, defended his commitment to Jewish safety while maintaining his stance on U.S. aid to Israel and Gaza, rejecting antisemitism claims. Pro-Israel groups, previously backing Stevens, indicated they would now support Rogers. El-Sayed's tight win, despite earlier double-digit poll leads, signifies another high-profile progressive primary success this cycle.","shortHeadline":"Democratic Party Unity","topic":"US Elections","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a73989c0a498617335160f4"},{"title":"Valspar Kicks Off 2027 Color Of The Year Season With ‘Cottage Door’","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/rdaniel-foster/2026/08/05/valspar-kicks-off-2027-color-of-the-year-season-with-cottage-door/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a72b33b1419b494c1d17944/960x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1200\u0026cropY1=41\u0026cropY2=717","author":"R. Daniel Foster","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/7241316","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/rdaniel-foster","authorBio":"R. Daniel Foster conveys the essence of art, culture and design for a global audience. Based in Los Angeles, his articles have appeared in the Wall Street Journal, Los Angeles Times, Harvard Business Review, Esquire and on National Public Radio. His interviews with James Cameron, Jodie Foster, Halle Berry, Ellen DeGeneres, John Travolta, Laura Dern and others have been read by a worldwide audience. His visual art has been featured by PBS and Los Angeles’ premier art institutions: the LA Opera, Hammer Museum, and the Center Theatre Group. National outlets include the Kennedy Center and Walker Art Center. Awards for his short films and documentaries include \"Best Experimental Film\" for the short Airborn, awarded by the International Festival of Cinema and Technology, and a \"Best Documentary\" nomination from the Asian Festival of 1st Films in Singapore. Follow Foster for ongoing coverage of fresh takes on singular art and trends.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/671287e01a996cdc4d86a9d8/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1400\u0026cropY1=111\u0026cropY2=1511","authorSlug":"rdaniel-foster","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Valspar Paints has launched the 2027 Color of the Year season, naming Cottage Door (8004-38E) as its selection. This mid-tone neutral blue is described as seductively serene, offering calm and an antidote to constant stimulation. Valspar's director, Sue Kim, noted its all-over neutrality, even using it in her bedroom for a cozy, anchored feel. Blue hues are a strong trend, appearing in 2026 reports from Houzz and WGSN/Coloro, which picked \"Luminous Blue\" for 2027. Major brands like Glidden, Sherwin-Williams, and Benjamin Moore have also recently chosen blue shades. Valspar itself previously selected Encore (2025) and Renew Blue, prior to its 2026 choice of Warm Eucalyptus.","shortHeadline":"Color of the Year","topic":"Interior Design","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/52720-6a72985ea313137abcda102f"},{"title":"Top Islay And Campbeltown Scotch Whisky, From The Global Spirits Masters","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/joemicallef/2026/08/05/top-islay--campbeltown-scotch-whisky-from-the-global-spirits-masters/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a693a9997304270c0925d5b/960x0.jpg","author":"Joseph V Micallef","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3239424","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/joemicallef/","authorBio":"Joseph V. Micallef is a journalist covering food, wine, spirits, and travel. He is also a historian, best-selling author, keynote speaker, and syndicated columnist. He has been a Forbes Contributor since 2017. Joe has written extensively on wines and spirits and judged many of the leading international wines and spirits competitions. In his spare time, he makes wine in Oregon. He holds the Wines and Spirits Diploma from the WSET and is an original member of the Advisory Board of the Council of Whiskey Masters. In 2023, the Academia Mexicana de Catadores de Tequila, Vino y Mezcal A.C. awarded him the honorific of Maestro Tequilero. he is also a Keeper of the Quaich. Among his recent books is Scotch Whisky: Its History, Production and Appreciation.","authorAvatar":"https://blogs-images.forbes.com/files/2017/11/joemicallef_avatar_1510775040-400x400.jpg","authorSlug":"joemicallef","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Campbeltown and Islay, despite their modest whisky output, consistently produce some of Scotland's most highly regarded single malts, a reputation strongly reinforced at the 2025 Global Spirits Masters. Campbeltown's Glen Scotia impressed across its core range and limited editions, earning a prestigious Tastemaster Award for its 2026 Malts Festival Edition, alongside Master Medals for its Harbor, 12-Year-Old, and 15-Year-Old expressions, all showcasing elegant maritime character. Islay's Kilchoman and the iconic Lagavulin also secured Master Medals, demonstrating the enduring appeal of expertly balanced peat smoke, coastal influence, and long maturation. Kilchoman's Rockside 11 Years Old highlighted its unique farm-to-bottle style, while Lagavulin 16-Year-Old exemplified its rich peat smoke and maritime elegance. These award-winning whiskies underscore the individuality and world-class quality defining Scotland's western whisky regions. Glen Scotia's performance confirms its growing reputation for refined maritime malts, while Kilchoman and Lagavulin solidify Islay's status as the home of Scotland's most expressive peated whiskies, showcasing diverse and characterful styles.","shortHeadline":"Top Scotch Whiskies","topic":"Fine Spirits","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/5354-6a68dfb6bcd78378c6fb75a0"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/ZThe3snJ-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"ZThe3snJ","audioDurationS":272,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-06T06:30:37-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-05-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-05","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Wednesday, August 5. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Politics, Lobbying, and Astronomy.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Politics space, we start with, Bernie moreno accuses max miller of holding granddaughter’s bunny ‘hostage’. According to reporting from Sara Dorn\n\nSenator Bernie Moreno, R-Ohio, publicly lambasted Representative Max Miller, R-Ohio, on Tuesday, detailing a contentious custody exchange where Miller allegedly refused to return Moreno's granddaughter's beloved \"little blue bunny.\" Moreno described the incident as an act of \"total and complete depravity,\" stating Miller \"held it hostage for 48 hours,\" causing the two-year-old significant distress. The custody transfer, which Moreno handled at a suburban Cleveland police station due to fears about Miller's behavior, underscores the ongoing, heated dispute between Moreno's daughter and Miller. Moreno reiterated his call for Miller's resignation, asserting that Miller \"fails to meet that minimum basic standard\" for public office and that his daughter \"lives in constant fear of this man.\" This public escalation follows Miller's recent video statement denying abuse allegations from Moreno's daughter. Further developments Tuesday included Miller's attorney accepting responsibility for publishing an unclothed photo of their daughter in documents Miller released to support his innocence claims. Additionally, the House Ethics Committee announced an investigation into Miller, a probe he had requested himself to clear his name amidst the allegations.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, Sean duffy’s road trip sponsors spent $100 million lobbying congress last year, a story that could have significant implications for Lobbying.\n\nSean Duffy's \"The Great American Road Trip\" YouTube series, intended to inspire travel for America's 250th anniversary, remains unreleased despite a promoted June launch. Corporate sponsors, including Boeing and Toyota who each contributed $1 million, collectively spent an estimated $5 million on the project. These same companies, however, spent over $100 million lobbying Congress in 2025. The delay has drawn critics who highlight that many sponsors are regulated by Duffy's Department of Transportation, raising concerns about potential conflicts of interest and corporate influence.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, How a village of 66 in spain is preparing for the eclipse — by closing its mountain. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Astronomy.\n\nSpain is preparing for its first total solar eclipse since 1905 on August 12, with a tiny village at the center of a debate over sustainable tourism. Jabaloyas, home to just 66 residents, boasts El Javalón, a highly sought-after viewing location due to the eclipse's low position in the sky. However, its mayor has controversially decided to close vehicle access to the peak, prioritizing public safety and environmental protection over potential economic gain. The decision stems from fears that the village's limited infrastructure cannot handle thousands of visitors, posing risks like traffic gridlock preventing emergency services and increased wildfire danger in the UNESCO-recognized Valle del Cabriel Biosphere Reserve. The mayor, Óscar Castillo, emphasized that the mountain road was not built for such traffic, and a small municipality lacks resources for safe management. While acknowledging the desire for local businesses to benefit, Castillo stressed that safety is paramount and \"not up for sale.\" Visitors can still walk to El Javalón, and other viewing spots exist, promoting a more sustainable approach to tourism. This event highlights the challenge of balancing global astrotourism with the preservation of small, remote communities, especially with two more eclipses following in 2027 and 2028.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"Bernie Moreno Accuses Max Miller Of Holding Granddaughter’s Bunny ‘Hostage’","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/08/04/bernie-moreno-accuses-max-miller-of-holding-granddaughters-bunny-hostage/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a722c5a7ca992c012db0c9d/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Senator Bernie Moreno, R-Ohio, publicly lambasted Representative Max Miller, R-Ohio, on Tuesday, detailing a contentious custody exchange where Miller allegedly refused to return Moreno's granddaughter's beloved \"little blue bunny.\" Moreno described the incident as an act of \"total and complete depravity,\" stating Miller \"held it hostage for 48 hours,\" causing the two-year-old significant distress. The custody transfer, which Moreno handled at a suburban Cleveland police station due to fears about Miller's behavior, underscores the ongoing, heated dispute between Moreno's daughter and Miller. Moreno reiterated his call for Miller's resignation, asserting that Miller \"fails to meet that minimum basic standard\" for public office and that his daughter \"lives in constant fear of this man.\" This public escalation follows Miller's recent video statement denying abuse allegations from Moreno's daughter. Further developments Tuesday included Miller's attorney accepting responsibility for publishing an unclothed photo of their daughter in documents Miller released to support his innocence claims. Additionally, the House Ethics Committee announced an investigation into Miller, a probe he had requested himself to clear his name amidst the allegations.","shortHeadline":"Political Accusations","topic":"Politics","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a72