/sites/jamiecartereurope","authorBio":"Jamie Carter is an award-winning reporter, author, astrotourism guide, eclipse chaser, aurora hunter and stargazer who covers the night sky and space exploration. The world’s foremost solar eclipse journalist and eclipse, Carter edits WhenIsTheNextEclipse.com and works on eclipse and other astrotourism-themed trips for New Scientist Discovery Tours. The co-author of The Eclipse Effect (2026), Complete Guide to the Total Solar Eclipse 2026 (2025) and Complete Guide to the Total Solar Eclipse 2027 (2026), and author of Chasing the Northern Lights (2026), Stargazing In 2026 and A Stargazing Program for Beginners (2015), he received the 2024 Popular Media Award from the American Astronomical Society’s Solar Physics Division for his coverage of the 2024 total solar eclipse. Writing for Forbes.com since 2018, Carter has also reported on the intense geomagnetic storms and global northern lights in 2024-25, incoming comets, the sun’s solar maximum and the search for intelligent life in the cosmos. He also writes for Space.com, Live Science, Sky \u0026 Telescope, BBC Sky At Night and New Scientist.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/60405466b33286b65965b0ce/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"jamiecartereurope","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Spain is preparing for its first total solar eclipse since 1905 on August 12, with a tiny village at the center of a debate over sustainable tourism. Jabaloyas, home to just 66 residents, boasts El Javalón, a highly sought-after viewing location due to the eclipse's low position in the sky. However, its mayor has controversially decided to close vehicle access to the peak, prioritizing public safety and environmental protection over potential economic gain. The decision stems from fears that the village's limited infrastructure cannot handle thousands of visitors, posing risks like traffic gridlock preventing emergency services and increased wildfire danger in the UNESCO-recognized Valle del Cabriel Biosphere Reserve. The mayor, Óscar Castillo, emphasized that the mountain road was not built for such traffic, and a small municipality lacks resources for safe management. While acknowledging the desire for local businesses to benefit, Castillo stressed that safety is paramount and \"not up for sale.\" Visitors can still walk to El Javalón, and other viewing spots exist, promoting a more sustainable approach to tourism. This event highlights the challenge of balancing global astrotourism with the preservation of small, remote communities, especially with two more eclipses following in 2027 and 2028.","shortHeadline":"Eclipse Preparations","topic":"Astronomy","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/6117-6a724277838fd3fed3a8a8b3"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/zBucJ925-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"zBucJ925","audioDurationS":260,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-05T06:30:36-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-08-04-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-08-04","script":"You're listening to The Daily Brief, your essential news update from Forbes. It's Tuesday, August 4. Today, we're highlighting the key stories covering Education, Hospitality, and a closer look at Real Estate.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nLeading off with, This steven spielberg-backed nonprofit gets teens out of their cultural bubbles. Lisa Chambers has been tracking this development and reports that\n\nForbes 30 Under 30 alum David McCullough III cofounded American Exchange Project in 2019 with an ambitious goal: making two-week domestic exchanges between high school graduation and college as common as prom. Inspired by his late grandfather's belief that everyone has something to teach, McCullough developed the idea after concluding that the largely unstructured summer after high school is one of the most overlooked opportunities in education. The nonprofit has since grown to 30 employees and attracted more than $30 million in philanthropic commitments, including a more than $14 million pledge from Steven Spielberg and Kate Capshaw's Hearthland Foundation. Participants like Louisiana-born Allonah Ashworth say the experience reshaped their futures; after her exchange, she changed career paths, launched cultural programming at her university, studied abroad and is now pursuing a master's degree in mental health counseling. Drawing on his own cross-country travels and research into exchange programs, McCullough believes firsthand experiences can broaden perspectives and strengthen connections across America's social and geographic divides.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, Sheraton stockholm renovation creates a new lifestyle destination, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in Hospitality.\n\nThe Sheraton Stockholm has re-emerged as a premier Nordic destination after a $45 million overhaul, aiming to reinvent the hotel with a lifestyle soul. Located on the waterfront between the Old Town and the modern city center, its 463 spacious rooms boast modern Scandinavian design and locally sourced amenities, while some offer stunning Lake Mälaren views. General Manager Elin Roquet emphasizes blending reliability and warmth with a contemporary feel, reflecting Stockholm's natural beauty. Public spaces include Sally's, a buzzing lobby bar with inventive cocktails, and Mr. Bronck, a lively steakhouse. A wellness center offers cutting-edge fitness and biohacking therapies like cryotherapy for guests and locals. Nearly 10,000 square feet of event space accommodates diverse gatherings, while the concierge team curates insider itineraries, guiding guests to cultural highlights like Fotografiska and Moderna Museet. Shopping options range from SoFo's design district to luxury brands in Bibliotekstan. Nearby, the vibrant G.A.T. bistro offers elevated comfort food and a lively atmosphere.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, The cost of weather is reshaping how americans choose where to relocate. This headline is generating significant interest within Real Estate.\n\nRemote work gave Americans more freedom to choose where they live. Now, weather is becoming part of the relocation equation, as buyers weigh insurance costs, flood risk, resilience, and the long-term cost of homeownership alongside lifestyle and affordability. The result is a broader definition of what makes a place worth calling home—and what it may truly cost over time.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"This Steven Spielberg-Backed Nonprofit Gets Teens Out Of Their Cultural Bubbles","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/lisachambers/2026/08/03/this-steven-spielberg-backed-nonprofit-gets-teens-out-of-their-cultural-bubbles/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a709645ec65bf4071188244/960x0.jpg","author":"Lisa Chambers","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/7246234","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/lisachambers","authorBio":"Lisa Chambers is a Forbes staff writer covering higher education. She previously served as the managing editor of the Fiske Guide to Colleges, where she reported on admissions, affordability, student outcomes and global higher education trends. She has also written for institutions including Morehouse School of Medicine and the University of Delaware. Earlier in her career, she held senior editorial roles at TV Guide and Centennial Media. Raised on campuses as the daughter of a college president, she now lives in New York’s Hudson Valley.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/69f907dab399822ba0d36717/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=588\u0026cropY1=51\u0026cropY2=639","authorSlug":"lisachambers","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Forbes 30 Under 30 alum David McCullough III cofounded American Exchange Project in 2019 with an ambitious goal: making two-week domestic exchanges between high school graduation and college as common as prom. Inspired by his late grandfather's belief that everyone has something to teach, McCullough developed the idea after concluding that the largely unstructured summer after high school is one of the most overlooked opportunities in education. The nonprofit has since grown to 30 employees and attracted more than $30 million in philanthropic commitments, including a more than $14 million pledge from Steven Spielberg and Kate Capshaw's Hearthland Foundation. Participants like Louisiana-born Allonah Ashworth say the experience reshaped their futures; after her exchange, she changed career paths, launched cultural programming at her university, studied abroad and is now pursuing a master's degree in mental health counseling. Drawing on his own cross-country travels and research into exchange programs, McCullough believes firsthand experiences can broaden perspectives and strengthen connections across America's social and geographic divides.","shortHeadline":"Teen Cultural Exchange","topic":"Education","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/53247-6a6bb50eb8bc52d6b673383e"},{"title":"Sheraton Stockholm Renovation Creates A New Lifestyle Destination","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/alexandrakirkman/2026/08/03/sheraton-stockholm-renovation-creates-a-new-lifestyle-destination/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a674e11d347a9aa6e48adf6/960x0.jpg","author":"Alexandra Kirkman","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/1485595","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/alexandrakirkman/","authorBio":"Alexandra Kirkman is a New York-based freelance writer who covers travel, with a focus on luxury properties and experiences and singular destinations. A contributor since 2014, she’s written about Italy’s best escap