esimg.com/imageserve/6a6be01b2d33dc4aa07f40ac/960x0.jpg","author":"Celia Shatzman","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/1255008","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/celiashatzman/","authorBio":"Some kids dream of becoming President of the United States or going into outer space. But Celia always dreamed of being a writer. That’s why she started her own lifestyle magazine in first grade. That publication folded, but it didn’t deter her from pursuing journalism. She has written for New York, Harper's Bazaar, Cosmopolitan, NYLON, Byrdie, ELLE, Glamour, Marie Claire, Teen Vogue, SELF, New York Post, USA TODAY, Conde Nast Traveler, Health, BBC Travel, Latina and Organic Spa Magazine, among others. She is a graduate of the Medill School of Journalism at Northwestern University and calls Brooklyn home.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/603e3cd32da1e286e83f4562/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"celiashatzman","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Sofwave is a non-invasive skin-tightening device using high-frequency ultrasound to lift and firm skin on the face, neck and body. It stands out for its zero downtime, making it popular even in summer, as it doesn't break the skin barrier and keeps the surface cool despite internal heating, eliminating sun exposure risks. Patients can immediately resume normal activities. Sofwave stimulates collagen, elastin and hyaluronic acid, improving firmness, reducing fine lines and addressing crepey skin and acne scars without fat loss or nerve damage. Typically, one treatment per year is recommended, often integrated into a comprehensive anti-aging regimen alongside other procedures, even on the same day. Experts suggest starting in the late 30s or early 40s to proactively combat collagen loss and maintain skin quality. Results, including lifting and tightening, become visible over 8-12 weeks as collagen builds, lasting about a year. While not painless—patients may experience a \"hot pinch\" during treatment, managed with cooling or numbing cream—the discomfort is temporary, and the results are consistent and long-lasting across all skin types.","shortHeadline":"Skin Treatments","topic":"Beauty","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/3247-6a6bdebdb00719d0a86d44ea"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/oeJmmYoX-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"oeJmmYoX","audioDurationS":263,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-08-01T08:30:41-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-31-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-31","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Friday, July 31. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Immigration, US Politics, and Campaign Finance.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Immigration space, we start with, TSA is helping ICE ramp up arrests at airports. According to reporting from Suzanne Rowan Kelleher\n\nThe Transportation Security Administration and Immigration and Customs Enforcement are collaborating to arrest foreign visitors at airports who have overstayed their U.S. visas, sparking concerns over data privacy. The New York Times revealed enforcement actions in at least 15 airports recently, targeting individuals including spouses of Americans. A May 2025 memo, obtained by American Oversight, details an agreement for ICE to share \"information with TSA to identify individuals of interest,\" with TSA reciprocating by sharing unspecified \"data elements.\" This initiative aligns with the Trump administration's intensified deportation agenda, aiming for thousands of daily arrests. Plain-clothes ICE agents are confronting travelers at check-in and arrival gates, sometimes filmed by fellow passengers. Immigration attorneys note that even those legally in the U.S. and in the asylum process have been detained, a development described as \"very, very new.\"\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, Trump’s low approval rating is getting even worse: here’s why, a story that could have significant implications for US Politics.\n\nPresident Donald Trump's net approval rating has plunged to a new second-term low of -20.6, according to Nate Silver's poll tracker. This significant drop follows two major polls released this week, both indicating all-time lows for his approval. A Quinnipiac University poll registered his approval at just 32%, while a CNN/SSRS poll showed 34%, matching his record low from his first term. These dismal figures are largely attributed to growing public dissatisfaction with his handling of the ongoing Iran conflict. The Quinnipiac poll revealed that only 34% of voters support the war, with 60% opposing it—the highest opposition recorded by the group. Furthermore, 54% believe the U.S. is not winning, and 55% feel the conflict hasn't been worthwhile. The CNN poll reinforced this sentiment, with 67% believing Trump's actions in Iran have harmed the U.S., and 74% stating he lacks a clear plan, a seven-point increase since March. The U.S. and Iran recently exchanged strikes, escalating fears of a broader regional conflict, including reported drone attacks in Egypt. Despite reports of Trump's exasperation with the war, he has expressed openness to negotiations while vowing continued attacks if an agreement isn't upheld.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, Musk reportedly spending up to $120 million helping GOP in midterms—after saying he ‘got a little too involved in politics’. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Campaign Finance.\n\nElon Musk's super PAC, America PAC, is set to spend up to $120 million to support Republican candidates in the upcoming midterm elections, The New York Times reported. This significant investment deepens Musk's alliance with the GOP, despite his previously volatile relationship with former President Donald Trump. The PAC plans to target Senate races in at least eight states, including Alaska, Iowa, and Ohio, alongside several House contests. Its strategy involves mobilizing Republican voters less likely to participate in non-presidential election years and executing a \"historic 'get out the vote' operation.\" This spending comes as America PAC faces scrutiny over a $1 million payment to Wisconsin voters last year, tied to a petition. Musk, the largest political donor in 2024, had previously denounced the GOP and vowed to start his own party, but has since mended ties with Trump. Republicans currently risk losing their slim majorities in Congress, partly due to Trump's declining poll numbers and voter dissatisfaction, with Democrats leading by eight points on the generic congressional ballot. Interestingly, Musk recently expressed regret for getting \"too involved in politics\" and indicated he would reduce future political spending.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"TSA Is Helping ICE Ramp Up Arrests At Airports","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/2026/07/30/tsa-help-ice-arrests-airports/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a6a317a4b6bb0c1acfcaae9/960x0.jpg","author":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3340630","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/","authorBio":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher covers travel for Forbes. Follow her on Bluesky @srkelleher.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/601b7235b35a3ce5939c28fe/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"suzannerowankelleher","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"The Transportation Security Administration and Immigration and Customs Enforcement are collaborating to arrest foreign visitors at airports who have overstayed their U.S. visas, sparking concerns over data privacy. The New York Times revealed enforcement actions in at least 15 airports recently, targeting individuals including spouses of Americans. A May 2025 memo, obtained by American Oversight, details an agreement for ICE to share \"information with TSA to identify individuals of interest,\" with TSA reciprocating by sharing unspecified \"data elements.\" This initiative aligns with the Trump administration's intensified deportation agenda, aiming for thousands of daily arrests. Plain-clothes ICE agents are confronting travelers at check-in and arrival gates, sometimes filmed by fellow passengers. Immigration attorneys note that even those legally in the U.S. and in the asylum process have been detained, a development described as \"very, very new.\"","shortHeadline":"Airport Immigration Enforcement","topic":"Immigration","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/7082-6a6a2f79217b3d7bd9c63d55"},{"title":"Trump’s Low Approval Rating Is Getting Even Worse: Here’s Why","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/07/30/trumps-low-approval-rating-is-getting-even-worse-heres-why/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a6bb076ec2874705d45ab13/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"President Donald Trump's net approval rating has plunged to a new second-term low of -20.6, according to Nate Silver's poll tracker. This significant drop follows two major polls released this week, both indicating all-time lows for his approval. A Quinnipiac University poll registered his approval at just 32%, while a CNN/SSRS poll showed 34%, matching his record low from his first term. These dismal figures are largely attributed to growing public dissatisfaction with his handling of the ongoing Iran conflict. The Quinnipiac poll revealed that only 34% of voters support the war, with 60% opposing it—the highest opposition recorded by the group. Furthermore, 54% believe the U.S. is not winning, and 55% feel the conflict hasn't been worthwhile. The CNN poll reinforced this sentiment, with 67% believing Trump's actions in Iran have harmed the U.S., and 74% stating he lacks a clear plan, a seven-point increase since March. The U.S. and Iran recently exchanged strikes, escalating fears of a broader regional conflict, including reported drone attacks in Egypt. Despite reports of Trump's exasperation with the war, he has expressed openness to negotiations while vowing continued attacks if an agreement isn't upheld.","shortHeadline":"Trump Approval Ratings","topic":"US Politics","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a6bb032bb40e599f17a787d"},{"title":"Musk Reportedly Spending Up To $120 Million Helping GOP In Midterms—After Saying He ‘Got A Little Too Involved In Politics’","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/07/30/musk-reportedly-spending-up-to-120-million-helping-gop-in-midterms-after-saying-he-got-a-little-too-involved-in-politics/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a6ba27c4f732cfbb14447d9/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Elon Musk's super PAC, America PAC, is set to spend up to $120 million to support Republican candidates in the upcoming midterm elections, The New York Times reported. This significant investment deepens Musk's alliance with the GOP, despite his previously volatile relationship with former President Donald Trump. The PAC plans to target Senate races in at least eight states, including Alaska, Iowa, and Ohio, alongside several House contests. Its strategy involves mobilizing Republican voters less likely to participate in non-presidential election years and executing a \"historic 'get out the vote' operation.\" This spending comes as America PAC faces scrutiny over a $1 million payment to Wisconsin voters last year, tied to a petition. Musk, the largest political donor in 2024, had previously denounced the GOP and vowed to start his own party, but has since mended ties with Trump. Republicans currently risk losing their slim majorities in Congress, partly due to Trump's declining poll numbers and voter dissatisfaction, with Democrats leading by eight points on the generic congressional ballot. Interestingly, Musk recently expressed regret for getting \"too involved in politics\" and indicated he would reduce future political spending.","shortHeadline":"Musk Political Donations","topic":"Campaign Finance","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a6ba178b8bc52d6b673383a"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/6XFfi6p6-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"6XFfi6p6","audioDurationS":295,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-07-31T06:30:43-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-30-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-30","script":"You're listening to The Daily Brief, your essential news update from Forbes. It's Thursday, July 30. Today, we're highlighting the key stories covering Immigration, Astronomy, and a closer look at Luxury Travel.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nLeading off with, TSA is helping ICE ramp up arrests at airports. Suzanne Rowan Kelleher has been tracking this development and reports that\n\nThe Transportation Security Administration and Immigration and Customs Enforcement are collaborating to arrest foreign visitors at airports who have overstayed their U.S. visas, sparking concerns over data privacy. The New York Times revealed enforcement actions in at least 15 airports recently, targeting individuals including spouses of Americans. A May 2025 memo, obtained by American Oversight, details an agreement for ICE to share \"information with TSA to identify individuals of interest,\" with TSA reciprocating by sharing unspecified \"data elements.\" This initiative aligns with the Trump administration's intensified deportation agenda, aiming for thousands of daily arrests. Plain-clothes ICE agents are confronting travelers at check-in and arrival gates, sometimes filmed by fellow passengers. Immigration attorneys note that even those legally in the U.S. and in the asylum process have been detained, a development described as \"very, very new.\"\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, In photos: full ‘buck moon’ rises to set up a total solar eclipse, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in Astronomy.\n\nThe Buck Moon, the eighth full moon of 2026, illuminated midsummer skies on Wednesday, July 29, offering photographers a classic warm-weather spectacle. Best viewed at moonrise just after sunset, this full moon appeared low in the Northern Hemisphere sky and often looked orange due to atmospheric scattering, an effect enhanced by the moon illusion. Its traditional name comes from male deer regrowing antlers. This celestial event served as a prelude to a dramatic August, featuring a total solar eclipse on August 12. This eclipse will be visible from parts of Greenland, Iceland, and Spain, with a significant partial eclipse seen across Western Europe and North America, requiring special glasses. Just hours after the solar eclipse, the annual Perseid meteor shower will peak, potentially offering 50 shooting stars per hour. The month concludes with a deep partial lunar eclipse on August 27, where 96% of the Sturgeon Moon will enter Earth's shadow, creating a reddish hue visible across North America.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, The new mykonos resort with a water limo and a basketball court carved from rock. This headline is generating significant interest within Luxury Travel.\n\nFouquet’s Mykonos, the first Greek hotel from Barrière, opened in June on Paraga Beach, featuring 60 suites and three villas. It masterfully blends Mykonos' high-energy scene with private luxury. Its subtle French-meets-Mediterranean design features low-volume buildings that integrate into the rugged landscape with rough stone, sandy plaster, and warm wood. Accommodations range from rooms to expansive villas, many with private pools or sea access. Amenities include an NBA-standard blond-wood basketball court, a 2,260 sq ft gym, and the Rock Spa, offering Dr. Barbara Sturm and Germaine de Capuccini treatments. Dining features ROKA Mykonos for contemporary Japanese, Éole for Greek-Mediterranean, and Le Bar for signature cocktails. Owner Ioannis Papalekas's art collection, including Dalí's *Triumphant Elephant*, adorns the hotel. A unique 'water limo' service offers complimentary hourly transfers to hotspots like Scorpios and Nammos, providing a glamorous alternative to island traffic and private excursions.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"TSA Is Helping ICE Ramp Up Arrests At Airports","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/2026/07/29/tsa-help-ice-arrests-airports/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a6a317a4b6bb0c1acfcaae9/960x0.jpg","author":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3340630","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/","authorBio":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher covers travel for Forbes. Follow her on Bluesky @srkelleher.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/601b7235b35a3ce5939c28fe/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"suzannerowankelleher","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"The Transportation Security Administration and Immigration and Customs Enforcement are collaborating to arrest foreign visitors a