d author of Chasing the Northern Lights (2026), Stargazing In 2026 and A Stargazing Program for Beginners (2015), he received the 2024 Popular Media Award from the American Astronomical Society’s Solar Physics Division for his coverage of the 2024 total solar eclipse. Writing for Forbes.com since 2018, Carter has also reported on the intense geomagnetic storms and global northern lights in 2024-25, incoming comets, the sun’s solar maximum and the search for intelligent life in the cosmos. He also writes for Space.com, Live Science, Sky \u0026 Telescope, BBC Sky At Night and New Scientist.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/60405466b33286b65965b0ce/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"jamiecartereurope","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"The Buck Moon, the eighth full moon of 2026, illuminated midsummer skies on Wednesday, July 29, offering photographers a classic warm-weather spectacle. Best viewed at moonrise just after sunset, this full moon appeared low in the Northern Hemisphere sky and often looked orange due to atmospheric scattering, an effect enhanced by the moon illusion. Its traditional name comes from male deer regrowing antlers. This celestial event served as a prelude to a dramatic August, featuring a total solar eclipse on August 12. This eclipse will be visible from parts of Greenland, Iceland, and Spain, with a significant partial eclipse seen across Western Europe and North America, requiring special glasses. Just hours after the solar eclipse, the annual Perseid meteor shower will peak, potentially offering 50 shooting stars per hour. The month concludes with a deep partial lunar eclipse on August 27, where 96% of the Sturgeon Moon will enter Earth's shadow, creating a reddish hue visible across North America.","shortHeadline":"Lunar and Solar Events","topic":"Astronomy","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/6117-6a425ef725d0fb7aa641c636"},{"title":"The New Mykonos Resort With A Water Limo And A Basketball Court Carved From Rock","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/forbestravelguide/2026/07/29/the-new-mykonos-resort-with-a-water-limo-and-a-basketball-court-carved-from-rock/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a6a1730aca56c142af96abb/960x0.jpg","author":"Melinda Sheckells","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/7238212","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/forbestravelguide/","authorBio":"I am an award-winning writer, editor and television correspondent specializing in luxury travel, hospitality, dining, drinking, art, spas and entertainment. I live and play in Las Vegas but canvas the world for once-in-lifetime travel experiences to share with those who desire awe-inspiring moments. I specialize in private clubs, secret entrances, chic touches and #IYKYK. I appreciate a less-than-five-minute check-in, an artful arrival amenity and a hotel bar that stays open late. My favorite places are Madrid, Stockholm, Seoul, Marfa, Dubai, Macau and Chile. Next on my list are Iceland, Norway, Cape Town and Champagne. My writing appears in The Hollywood Reporter, Business Traveler, Hospitality Design, People, Infatuation and Apple Music, among other publications. I host a weekly food segment on ABC called “Tasty Tuesday” and appeared on Vice Munchies as “the queen of Las Vegas.”","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/654136d4eb3e558ab06d41e3/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1856\u0026cropY1=113\u0026cropY2=1969","authorSlug":"melindasheckells","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Fouquet’s Mykonos, the first Greek hotel from Barrière, opened in June on Paraga Beach, featuring 60 suites and three villas. It masterfully blends Mykonos' high-energy scene with private luxury. Its subtle French-meets-Mediterranean design features low-volume buildings that integrate into the rugged landscape with rough stone, sandy plaster, and warm wood. Accommodations range from rooms to expansive villas, many with private pools or sea access. Amenities include an NBA-standard blond-wood basketball court, a 2,260 sq ft gym, and the Rock Spa, offering Dr. Barbara Sturm and Germaine de Capuccini treatments. Dining features ROKA Mykonos for contemporary Japanese, Éole for Greek-Mediterranean, and Le Bar for signature cocktails. Owner Ioannis Papalekas's art collection, including Dalí's *Triumphant Elephant*, adorns the hotel. A unique 'water limo' service offers complimentary hourly transfers to hotspots like Scorpios and Nammos, providing a glamorous alternative to island traffic and private excursions.","shortHeadline":"Mykonos Luxury Resort","topic":"Luxury Travel","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/1916-6a6a16f700a9566ff096f355"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/xEW5XDG7-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"xEW5XDG7","audioDurationS":259,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-07-30T06:30:40-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-29-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-29","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, the essential news digest from the Forbes newsroom. It's Wednesday, July 29. Today, we're exploring the stories shaping the global conversation—from the latest in Aerospace Industry and Political Scandals to a deep dive into Legal Affairs.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nWe begin with a look at, Boeing lost another $280 million last quarter on overdue air force one. Suzanne Rowan Kelleher has been following this story and reports that\n\nBoeing announced a $280 million second-quarter loss on its government contract to deliver two new Air Force One presidential aircraft, highlighting the inherent risks of fixed-price agreements. The project is significantly behind schedule, having missed its original 2024 delivery and now targeting 2028 for the first jet. Since the 2018 contract, Boeing has likely lost $2.8 billion on the program. The $3.9 billion fixed-price deal means Boeing, not the U.S. government, absorbs most cost overruns. The aircraft's extensive modifications, transforming it into a flying White House with secure global communications, missile defense, and aerial refueling, contribute to its immense complexity and escalating costs. Boeing's former CEO previously stated the company \"probably shouldn't have taken\" the contract. The latest $280 million overrun is attributed to additional production and certification resources, with executives expecting further cost increases.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou can find the full analysis of that story at Forbes.com. Now, we're turning our attention to, Gavin newsom downplayed affair with top aide's wife, she asserts in tell-all, a development that could have a lasting impact on Political Scandals.\n\nFormer aide Ruby Rippey, in a Vanity Fair essay, publicly challenged California Governor Gavin Newsom's portrayal of their past affair, disputing his memoir's \"stupidest and briefest\" description. Rippey, then married to Newsom's top staffer, characterized the late 2005 relationship as an \"intoxication\" linked to power, not love, and a \"fracture that split her life,\" contrasting with Newsom's \"footnote.\" She also contested his claim of \"coming clean\" to her then-husband, implying she had already disclosed it. Rippey revealed that then-San Francisco District Attorney Kamala Harris offered her crucial \"best advice\" against publicly discussing the affair for a memoir. Newsom's spokesperson reiterated the governor's nearly two-decade-old public apology, stating he has moved on with his family and career, offering no further comment. The affair, which Newsom admitted publicly after Rippey's husband confronted him, occurred while Newsom was divorcing his first wife. Both Newsom and Harris are now considered potential 2028 Democratic presidential candidates, adding political relevance to Rippey's renewed account.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRounding out today's briefing, we look at, What trump suing the BBC could reveal about his finances. This story is currently at the center of the conversation in Legal Affairs.\n\nPresident Donald Trump’s $10 billion lawsuit against the BBC could unintentionally force him to shed light on his own personal finances, as the trust holding the president’s assets was ordered Monday to turn over financial documents to the broadcaster in order to prove Trump’s business and reputation was damaged as much as he claims.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou've reached the end of this Daily Brief. Tune in again for tomorrow's updates. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":0,"articles":[{"title":"Boeing Lost Another $280 Million Last Quarter On Overdue Air Force One","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/2026/07/28/boeing-lost-another-280-million-air-force-one/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a68c33dd571530bf4fc28ac/960x0.jpg","author":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3340630","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/","authorBio":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher covers travel for Forbes. Follow her on Bluesky @srkelleher.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/601b7235b35a3ce5939c28fe/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"suzannerowankelleher","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Boeing announced a $280 million second-quarter loss on its government contract to deliver two new Air Force One presidential aircraft, highlighting the inherent risks of fixed-price agreements. The project is significantly behind schedule, having missed its original 2024 delivery and now targeting 2028 for the first jet. Since the 2018 contract, Boeing has likely lost $2.8 billion on the program. The $3.9 billion fixed-price deal means Boeing, not the U.S. government, absorbs most cost overruns. The aircraft's extensive modifications, transforming it into a flying White House with secure global communications, missile defense, and aerial refueling, contribute to its immense complexity and escalating costs. Boeing's former CEO previously stated the company \"probably shouldn't have taken\" the contract. The latest $280 million overrun is attributed to additional production and certification resources, with executives expecting further cost increases.","shortHeadline":"Boeing's Contract Losses","topic":"Aerospace Industry","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/7082-6a68c2b706a0475f11d0ad33"},{"title":"Gavin Newsom Downplayed Affair With Top Aide's Wife, She Asserts In Tell-All","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/07/28/gavin-newsom-downplayed-affair-with-top-aides-wife-she-asserts-in-tell-all/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a6912aba7ed994ab09d0c7a/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Former aide Ruby