ersity of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Former President Donald Trump has issued a direct rebuke to Senate Majority Leader John Thune, demanding the Senate not break for its August recess until the \"Save America Act\" is passed. Trump, in a Truth Social post, criticized Thune's leadership, stating Democrats were \"lucky\" with him and that \"stupidity always brings LOSING \u0026 DEATH!\" The \"Save America Act,\" which mandates documentary proof of citizenship for voter registration, remains stalled in the Senate. Its passage requires overcoming the 60-vote filibuster threshold, necessitating unlikely Democratic support. Even eliminating the filibuster itself would require 60 votes. White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt echoed Trump's sentiment, indicating his \"patience is running out\" with Thune and emphasizing the desire for the Act's passage before the recess, scheduled from August 7 to September 14. Senator Mike Lee has also advocated for a roll call vote to cancel the recess until the bill advances. Thune, however, pushed back, suggesting the White House should focus on convincing Democrats or even Republican senators like Susan Collins and Lisa Murkowski, who have opposed the bill, rather than blaming Republican leadership.","shortHeadline":"Trump-Thune Relationship","topic":"Politics","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a676cbb140c1601ee8a9974"},{"title":"The Most Expensive Lake Weekends In Canada Aren’t In Muskoka, New Analysis Finds","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/kaitlynmcinnis/2026/07/27/the-most-expensive-lake-weekends-in-canada-arent-in-muskoka-new-analysis-finds/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a67b3c01a895562fde58d0d/960x0.jpg","author":"Kaitlyn McInnis","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5000844","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/kaitlynmcinnis","authorBio":"I am an experienced writer, editor, and consultant splitting my time between Halifax, Canada, and Istanbul, Turkey. Formerly on the management teams of multiple leading national publications, I currently write about travel, lifestyle, luxury, and success for Conde Nast Traveler, Travel + Leisure, MAXIM magazine, Tatler Asia, Mashable, Robb Report Singapore, South China Morning Post, The Points Guy, and more. My travel writing has also been printed in dozens of international publications across six continents. I hold a Bachelor of Arts focused in English Literature and Irish Studies from Concordia University and a Master of Fine Arts in Creative Nonfiction Writing from the University of King's College. I'm also proficient in three languages. Reach me at kaitlyn.a.mcinnis@gmail.com or follow my carefully curated Instagram and Twitter adventures at @itskaitmcinnis.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/667c87fa4070ff92887ea954/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=560\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=560","authorSlug":"kaitlynmcinnis","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"A new study by Casinos Analyzer Canada reveals that a two-night cottage getaway in some of Canada's top lake destinations can be surprisingly costly, with Atlantic Canada now leading in expense. The analysis, which factored in cottage rentals, fuel, groceries, drinks, and firewood, found Nova Scotia to be the most expensive, averaging $1,347. This is largely due to accommodation costs exceeding $1,000 for two nights and high alcohol prices. Ontario's renowned Muskoka region, often perceived as the priciest, came in a close second at $1,308, with rentals around $974. British Columbia's Okanagan ranked third at $1,259, notable for its high fuel expenses despite slightly lower accommodation. Kawartha Lakes, Ontario, followed at $1,235, benefiting from shorter drives from Toronto. Quebec's Laurentians offered a more budget-friendly option at $1,192, partly due to cheaper groceries and alcohol, securing the fifth spot among the surveyed regions. Canadians planning summer lake escapes should budget accordingly, as costs vary significantly across the country.","shortHeadline":"Expensive Lake Weekends","topic":"Luxury Travel","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/50353-6a67b30fb2fa6cfbe9379a98"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/ETHgdhnI-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"ETHgdhnI","audioDurationS":294,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-07-28T06:30:42-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-27-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-27","script":"You're listening to The Daily Brief, your essential news update from Forbes. It's Monday, July 27. Today, we're highlighting the key stories covering Libraries, Wine, and a closer look at Travel.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nLeading off with, Helsinki’s oodi library is much more than a place for books. David Nikel has been tracking this development and reports that\n\nHelsinki's Oodi Central Library redefines the modern library, serving as a vibrant \"public living room\" rather than just a book repository. Its striking wooden exterior, designed by ALA Architects, makes it an architectural landmark. Inside, Oodi buzzes with diverse activities; children play, friends gather, people work, sew, record music, play video games, and utilize 3D printers, embodying Nordic design's principle of improving everyday life. Oodi's strong sense of public ownership stems from its design process, which incorporated over 2,000 ideas from Helsinki residents, ensuring it met community needs for quiet zones, creative spaces, and free gathering spots. This public involvement explains why Oodi feels genuinely used and cherished.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, Forget five more champagne myths to enjoy your wine more, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in Wine.\n\nThis article debunks common champagne myths, applicable to all sparkling wines. Firstly, serving quality champagne ice cold mutes its flavors; optimal enjoyment comes from serving it warmer, between 8-14°C, allowing aromas to develop. Secondly, traditional flute and coupe glasses are suboptimal for appreciating bouquet; tulip-shaped wine glasses are preferred. Thirdly, vintage champagne isn't always superior to non-vintage (NV); while vintage reflects yearly variations, NV offers consistent character, and prestige cuvées often serve as a revenue strategy. Fourthly, \"Champagne\" legally originates from France's Champagne region in the EU and most countries, though the US has grandfathered exceptions like \"California Champagne.\" Fifthly, claiming champagne is the \"world's best\" is subjective; many regions, including Cava, Franciacorta, and MCC, produce outstanding sparkling wines. Finally, the belief that abundant small bubbles signify quality is false; bubble formation depends on glass factors like cleanliness and etching, not the wine's inherent excellence. Dispelling these misconceptions enhances the enjoyment of sparkling wines.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, Priceline just named the best places in the U.S. to see fall foliage. This headline is generating significant interest within Travel.\n\nPlanning an autumn trip to witness vibrant fall foliage requires early preparation, as popular destinations book up quickly. Priceline analyzed hotel search data for 2026 to identify the top U.S. destinations generating interest for leaf-peeping, spanning New England to the Pacific Northwest. The Adirondacks, New York, claimed the top spot with its vast forests, scenic drives, and diverse fall activities like hot air balloon rides and Oktoberfest celebrations, offering one of the longest fall seasons. The Columbia River Gorge in Washington and Oregon ranked second, transforming into a tapestry of crimson and gold with cascading waterfalls like Multnomah Falls and the Hood River Fruit Loop. Aspen, Colorado, secured third place, renowned for its brilliant golden aspen groves, especially around Maroon Bells, offering hiking and mountain biking. Fourth was Bucks County, Pennsylvania, known for historic covered bridges, scenic drives, and the New Hope Railroad excursion. Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, rounded out the top five, offering breathtaking views from its Skyway Gondola and hiking in Franconia Notch State Park. Other notable destinations in Priceline's top ten included Michigan's Tunnel of Trees, North Lake Tahoe, The Berkshires, Stowe, Vermont, and French Lick, Indiana, highlighting America's broad autumn appeal.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"Helsinki’s Oodi Library Is Much More Than A Place For Books","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/davidnikel/2026/07/27/helsinkis-oodi-library-is-much-more-than-a-place-for-books/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a634bd35096e1574074fed3/960x0.jpg","author":"David Nikel","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3339225","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/davidnikel/","authorBio":"David Nikel is a British-Norwegian travel writer based in Norway, covering all things travel with a special focus on cruising and Scandinavia. A frequent cruiser, Nikel shares developments and trends from the global cruising industry. As a published guidebook author and owner of the website Life in Norway, he also shares travel stories from Scandinavia and the Nordic region. Follow Nikel to help get the most out of your next cruise vacation or trip to Northern Europe.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/5f46708af8880e38567fa9b8/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=224\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=224","authorSlug":"davidnikel","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Helsinki's Oodi Central Library redefines the modern library, serving as a vibrant \"public living room\" rather than just a book repository. Its striking wooden exterior, designed by ALA Architects, makes it an architectural landmark. Inside, Oodi buzzes with diverse activities; children play, friends gather, people work, sew, record music, play video games, and utilize 3D printers, embodying Nordic design's principle of improving everyday life. Oodi's strong sense of public ownership stems from its design process, which incorporated over 2,000 ideas from Helsinki residents, ensuring it met community needs for quiet zones, creative spaces, and free gathering spots. This public involvement explains why Oodi feels genuinely used and cherished.","shortHeadline":"Library Innovations","topic":"Libraries","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/6560-6a6348f75d2e9eda9d6a10ef"},{"title":"Forget Five More Champagne Myths To Enjoy Your Wine More","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/karlsson/2026/07/26/forget-five-more-champagne-myths-to-enjoy-your-wine-more/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a65dad1fdccb31591d026f1/960x0.jpg","author":"Per and Britt Karlsson","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/1561220","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/karlsson/","authorBio":"Britt \u0026 Per Karlsson write on wine, wine travel and the business of wine, on Forbes.com since 2015. They publish BKWine Magazine, an online wine magazine. They own and operate BKWine Tours that have been named “World’s Top Wine Tours” and “World’s Best Wine Guide”. On Forbes.com they cover the global wine scene based on their extensive travel to wine regions, visiting hundreds of wineries every year. Their more than ten books on wine have won several literary awards from e.g. the International Organisation of Vine and Wine-OIV and Gourmand International. They are frequently judges in international wine competitions and are wine editors for the National Encyclopedia in Sweden. This takes them travelling in wine regions all around the world. Follow the Karlssons if you want to know more about the evolution of the world of wine and discover more on wine and wine tourism.","authorAvatar":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/f29e98b64eca022f1d11b39a4bdabf88?s=400\u0026d=mm\u0026r=g","authorSlug":"karlsson","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"This article debunks common champagne myths, applicable to all sparkling wines. Firstly, serving quality champagne ice cold mutes its flavors; optimal enjoyment comes from serving it warmer, between 8-14°C, allowing aromas to develop. Secondly, traditional flute and coupe glasses are suboptimal for appreciating bouquet; tulip-shaped wine glasses are preferred. Thirdly, vintage champagne isn't always superior to non-vintage (NV); while vintage reflects yearly variations, NV offers consistent character, and prestige cuvées often serve as a revenue strategy. Fourthly, \"Champagne\" legally originates from France's Champagne region in the EU and most countries, though the US has grandfathered exceptions like \"California Champagne.\" Fifthly, claiming champagne is the \"world's best\" is subjective; many regions, including Cava, Franciacorta, and MCC, produce outstanding sparkling wines. Finally, the belief that abundant small bubbles signify quality is false; bubble formation depends on glass factors like cleanliness and etching, not the wine's inherent excellence. Dispelling these misconceptions enhances the enjoyment of sparkling wines.","shortHeadline":"Champagne Myths","topic":"Wine","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/3567-6a65d85ce0a1040e2460659e"},{"title":"Priceline Just Named The Best Places In The U.S. To See Fall Foliage","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/brittanyanas/2026/07/27/priceline-just-named-the-best-places-in-the-us-to-see-fall-foliage/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a66f0007bcefc1b7cb377ca/960x0.jpg","author":"Brittany Anas","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3341372","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/brittanyanas/","authorBio":"I was struck with wanderlust at an early age. As a curious kid, I’d go to the library and flip through the atlas and encyclopedias, imagining I was traveling to far-off places. My grandma bought my fictional travel stories for $1 apiece. After graduating from the University of Colorado and working as the editor-in-chief of the campus newspaper, I spent more than a decade in daily newspapers, reporting and writing. I also did a brief stint as a federal background investigator before becoming a full-time travel and lifestyle writer. I’ve been a contributor to publications like Men’s Journal, American Way, Apartment Therapy, Simplemost, Better, TripSavvy, Livability, AAA publications, Reader’s Digest, The Denver Post, Yoga Journal, 5280, and more. I’m based in Denver, Colo., and I enjoy writing about adventure travel and the great outdoors. I also love finding quirky travel stories, and I am always on the hunt for a great spicy margarita.","authorAvatar":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/c50cde181f88ecd3f2a5cb1e6ec27375?s=400\u0026d=mm\u0026r=g","authorSlug":"brittanyanas","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Planning an autumn trip to witness vibrant fall foliage requires early preparation, as popular destinations book up quickly. Priceline analyzed hotel search data for 2026 to identify the top U.S. destinations generating interest for leaf-peeping, spanning New England to the Pacific Northwest. The Adirondacks, New York, claimed the top spot with its vast forests, scenic drives, and diverse fall activities like hot air balloon rides and Oktoberfest celebrations, offering one of the longest fall seasons. The Columbia River Gorge in Washington and Oregon ranked second, transforming into a tapestry of crimson and gold with cascading waterfalls like Multnomah Falls and the Hood River Fruit Loop. Aspen, Colorado, secured third place, renowned for its brilliant golden aspen groves, especially around Maroon Bells, offering hiking and mountain biking. Fourth was Bucks County, Pennsylvania, known for historic covered bridges, scenic drives, and the New Hope Railroad excursion. Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, rounded out the top five, offering breathtaking views from its Skyway Gondola and hiking in Franconia Notch State Park. Other notable destinations in Priceline's top ten included Michigan's Tunnel of Trees, North Lake Tahoe, The Berkshires, Stowe, Vermont, and French Lick, Indiana, highlighting America's broad autumn appeal.","shortHeadline":"Fall Foliage","topic":"Travel","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/7265-6a66ed477c3fa8370b6091b7"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/XszPkDBE-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"XszPkDBE","audioDurationS":275,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-07-27T06:30:42-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-25-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-25","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, the essential news digest from the Forbes newsroom. It's Saturday, July 25. Today, we're exploring the stories shaping the global conversation—from the latest in Air Travel and Kitchen Tech to a deep dive into Travel.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nWe begin with a look at, Surging jet fuel costs mean higher airfares are coming, experts warn. Suzanne Rowan Kelleher has been following this story and reports that\n\nJet fuel prices are sharply rising again, with the global average soaring nearly 18% to $149.40 per barrel by mid-July, reversing a brief stabilization in June. Major carriers like American, Southwest, Delta, United, and Alaska Airlines are reporting billions in increased fuel expenses, forcing many to cut their financial outlooks for the year. Experts warn that jet fuel could reach $200 a barrel if Middle East conflicts escalate, further depleting strategic reserves.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou can find the full analysis of that story at Forbes.com. Now, we're turning our attention to, The $400 toaster oven taking over professional kitchens, a development that could have a lasting impact on Kitchen Tech.\n\nThe best chocolate soufflé in Los Angeles, found at Michelin-starred Pasjoli, is surprisingly crafted in a Breville Smart Oven, a high-end countertop appliance typically used by home cooks. This revelation highlights a growing trend where professional kitchens, including those run by acclaimed chefs like Dave Beran, are increasingly relying on these versatile toaster ovens. Beran, known for his meticulous kitchen design, discovered the Breville baked more consistent soufflés than his expensive commercial combi, convection, and static ovens. Consequently, he now employs 8-10 Breville units across his restaurants, valuing their high performance, mobility, and cost-effectiveness, despite their non-commercial warranty. Chefs praise the Breville's precise Element IQ temperature control, super-convection fan, and multi-functionality, allowing it to proof, bake, roast, air-fry, and dehydrate. Its portability and ability to plug into standard outlets also make it ideal for dynamic kitchen environments. The Smart Oven Air Fryer Pro and Joule Oven Air Fryer Pro are the favored models among professionals.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRounding out today's briefing, we look at, These are the best airport lounges worldwide, per new analysis. This story is currently at the center of the conversation in Travel.\n\nAirport lounge access can significantly enhance travel, but a poor experience can be equally frustrating, leading travelers to prefer gates or airport bars. To help discerning travelers, a new analysis by Capital on Tap identified the world's top airport lounges, evaluating them based on Priority Pass ratings, operating hours, and available amenities. The study revealed five standout locations offering superior service and comfort. Topping the list is the Vienna Lounge at Vienna International Airport, scoring 8.73 out of ten, praised for its near-perfect Priority Pass rating and diverse amenities including a cinema, daybeds, and shoe-shine services. Following closely is the Goldair Handling Lounge Extra at Athens International Airport, securing second place with an 8.19 score, noted for its extensive 168 weekly operating hours and relaxed dress code. The Chase Sapphire Lounge by The Club at Harry Reid International Airport in Las Vegas also achieved an 8.19 score, ranking third with 15 amenities. The IGA Lounge International at Istanbul Airport came in fourth with a 7.94 score, distinguished by its 24/7 operation and unique bakery offerings. Finally, the Virgin Atlantic Clubhouse at Los Angeles International Airport rounded out the top five, scoring 7.64 and boasting 18 amenities, making it a prime choice for travelers seeking comfort before their flight.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou've reached the end of this Daily Brief. Tune in again for tomorrow's updates. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":0,"articles":[{"title":"Surging Jet Fuel Costs Mean Higher Airfares Are Coming, Experts Warn","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/2026/07/24/surging-jet-fuel-costs-higher-airfares/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a635da3171519e864124c1e/960x0.jpg","author":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3340630","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/","authorBio":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher covers travel for Forbes. Follow her on Bluesky @srkelleher.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/601b7235b35a3ce5939c28fe/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"suzannerowankelleher","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Jet fuel prices are sharply rising again, with the global average soaring nearly 18% to $149.40 per barrel by mid-July, reversing a brief stabilization in June. Major carriers like American, Southwest, Delta, United, and Alaska Airlines are reporting billions in increased fuel expenses, forcing many to cut their financial outlooks for the year. Experts warn that jet fuel could reach $200 a barrel if Middle East conflicts escalate, further depleting strategic reserves.","shortHeadline":"Rising Airfares","topic":"Air Travel","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/7082-6a635b5d7f8bf582325a0703"},{"title":"The $400 Toaster Oven Taking Over Professional Kitchens","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/davidhochman/2026/07/24/the-400-toaster-oven-taking-over-professional-kitchens/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a63f766f4a4bd8d1cd208d9/960x0.jpg","author":"David Hochman","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/548308","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/davidhochman/","authorBio":"David Hochman is an award-winning journalist providing a worldview from Los Angeles on luxury, travel, and entertainment. A Forbes writer (in print and online) since the early aughts, he explores epic experiences, unique destinations, luxury products, and profiles of passionate personalities in these fields--as in this Forbes print article on traveling the world by private jet. But concerts and culinary popups at iconic L.A. venues are also his jam. His bylines surface in The New York Times, GQ, Food + Wine, Town + Country and other venues. Hochman graduated from Columbia Journalism School with a Pulitzer traveling fellowship, and from Vassar College as an English major. Follow Hochman for continued coverage on what's delicious, refreshing, inspiring, indulgent and entertaining in California and way beyond.","authorAvatar":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b2b9b8bc60d