ntly write about travel, lifestyle, luxury, and success for Conde Nast Traveler, Travel + Leisure, MAXIM magazine, Tatler Asia, Mashable, Robb Report Singapore, South China Morning Post, The Points Guy, and more. My travel writing has also been printed in dozens of international publications across six continents. I hold a Bachelor of Arts focused in English Literature and Irish Studies from Concordia University and a Master of Fine Arts in Creative Nonfiction Writing from the University of King's College. I'm also proficient in three languages. Reach me at kaitlyn.a.mcinnis@gmail.com or follow my carefully curated Instagram and Twitter adventures at @itskaitmcinnis.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/667c87fa4070ff92887ea954/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=560\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=560","authorSlug":"kaitlynmcinnis","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Airport lounge access can significantly enhance travel, but a poor experience can be equally frustrating, leading travelers to prefer gates or airport bars. To help discerning travelers, a new analysis by Capital on Tap identified the world's top airport lounges, evaluating them based on Priority Pass ratings, operating hours, and available amenities. The study revealed five standout locations offering superior service and comfort. Topping the list is the Vienna Lounge at Vienna International Airport, scoring 8.73 out of ten, praised for its near-perfect Priority Pass rating and diverse amenities including a cinema, daybeds, and shoe-shine services. Following closely is the Goldair Handling Lounge Extra at Athens International Airport, securing second place with an 8.19 score, noted for its extensive 168 weekly operating hours and relaxed dress code. The Chase Sapphire Lounge by The Club at Harry Reid International Airport in Las Vegas also achieved an 8.19 score, ranking third with 15 amenities. The IGA Lounge International at Istanbul Airport came in fourth with a 7.94 score, distinguished by its 24/7 operation and unique bakery offerings. Finally, the Virgin Atlantic Clubhouse at Los Angeles International Airport rounded out the top five, scoring 7.64 and boasting 18 amenities, making it a prime choice for travelers seeking comfort before their flight.","shortHeadline":"Airport Lounge Rankings","topic":"Travel","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/50353-6a6388725231af7d32fd4217"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/HEwFVa2e-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"HEwFVa2e","audioDurationS":256,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-07-25T08:30:48-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-24-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-24","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Friday, July 24. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Foreign Policy, Foreign Policy, and Foreign Policy.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Foreign Policy space, we start with, House—Again—Votes to limit trump’s war powers in latest iran war rebuke. According to reporting from Sara Dorn\n\nThe House of Representatives voted 214-208 to block President Trump from continuing the war with Iran without congressional approval, a measure supported by four Republicans. This largely symbolic vote, which won't become law, comes amid a rising U.S. service member death toll, now at 18, and growing discontent among Trump's base. A recent Politico poll revealed a 13-point drop in MAGA voters who believe the war's economic costs are justified, highlighting the conflict's intersection with upcoming midterm elections. The Pentagon, having spent $37.5 billion, is now seeking an additional $67 billion in immediate funding for Middle East operations. Despite Trump's claim that Americans aren't against the war, the administration is preparing for a prolonged conflict. Trump escalated attacks after Iran refused to cede control of the Strait of Hormuz, unraveling a June agreement and declaring the ceasefire over. Iran responded by attacking U.S. troops, while Iran-backed Houthi rebels have expanded the conflict by attacking Saudi ships, prompting Trump to threaten retaliation. The Senate is also expected to vote on a similar war powers measure.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, Trump considering iran attack ‘bigger than ever before’, a story that could have significant implications for Foreign Policy.\n\nPresident Donald Trump told Axios he is \"close\" to deciding on a \"massive attack\" against Iran, potentially exceeding February's initial strikes. He stated, \"we're all set for it,\" believing Iranians \"want to negotiate\" but \"haven't received enough pain yet.\" This threat emerges as the House voted to restrict Trump's war powers, signaling political opposition. Concurrently, Brent crude oil prices surpassed $100 a barrel for the first time since May. Trump also escalated warnings to Iran-backed Houthi rebels, promising \"major military punishment\" for continued Red Sea attacks. Secretary of State Marco Rubio reinforced the administration's position, asserting Iran consistently breaks deals. Rubio added that the \"price will continue to get higher\" until they comply. Oil prices have climbed 40% since February, largely due to Houthi disruptions to vital shipping lanes. The U.S. and Iran have traded attacks for nearly 14 days after a memorandum on Strait of Hormuz control unraveled, with Trump previously threatening infrastructure bombings.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, Trump says saudi nuclear deal will ‘be approved’—if kingdom normalizes relations with israel. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Foreign Policy.\n\nPresident Donald Trump announced Thursday that the newly unveiled nuclear agreement with Saudi Arabia is contingent on the kingdom recognizing Israel and explicitly prohibits uranium enrichment. This statement directly contradicts earlier reports suggesting the deal would not require Israel's recognition and would permit Saudi Arabia to enrich uranium, possibly after a joint study. Trump clarified the U.S. supports \"Civil (Non-Enriched) Nuclear Facilities.\" The agreement, signed by U.S. Energy Secretary Chris Wright and Saudi Prince Abdulaziz bin Salman, reportedly allows Saudi Arabia to enrich its own fuel for reactors, with U.S. companies supplying the technology. However, concerns arise as the deal reportedly lacks the stringent IAEA inspection protocols seen in similar agreements, like with the UAE, which also forewent domestic fuel production. This 30-year deal marks a significant win for Saudi Arabia, pursued for years, yet it fuels fears of nuclear proliferation, especially given Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman's past warnings of seeking nuclear weapons if Iran does. The agreement surfaces amid strained U.S.-Saudi relations, partly due to the Iran war and Saudi Arabia's refusal to host U.S. attacks on Iran. Congress has 90 in-session days to approve the agreement, which is widely expected to pass.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"House—Again—Votes To Limit Trump’s War Powers In Latest Iran War Rebuke","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/07/23/house-again-votes-to-limit-trumps-war-powers-in-latest-iran-war-rebuke/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a622b2475e4a7b8deea11f8/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investiga