 races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"President Donald Trump announced Thursday that the newly unveiled nuclear agreement with Saudi Arabia is contingent on the kingdom recognizing Israel and explicitly prohibits uranium enrichment. This statement directly contradicts earlier reports suggesting the deal would not require Israel's recognition and would permit Saudi Arabia to enrich uranium, possibly after a joint study. Trump clarified the U.S. supports \"Civil (Non-Enriched) Nuclear Facilities.\" The agreement, signed by U.S. Energy Secretary Chris Wright and Saudi Prince Abdulaziz bin Salman, reportedly allows Saudi Arabia to enrich its own fuel for reactors, with U.S. companies supplying the technology. However, concerns arise as the deal reportedly lacks the stringent IAEA inspection protocols seen in similar agreements, like with the UAE, which also forewent domestic fuel production. This 30-year deal marks a significant win for Saudi Arabia, pursued for years, yet it fuels fears of nuclear proliferation, especially given Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman's past warnings of seeking nuclear weapons if Iran does. The agreement surfaces amid strained U.S.-Saudi relations, partly due to the Iran war and Saudi Arabia's refusal to host U.S. attacks on Iran. Congress has 90 in-session days to approve the agreement, which is widely expected to pass.","shortHeadline":"Saudi Nuclear Deal","topic":"Foreign Policy","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a620cb575557544aa600db7"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/RWvgD7S4-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"RWvgD7S4","audioDurationS":283,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-07-24T06:30:42-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-23-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-23","script":"You're listening to The Daily Brief, your essential news update from Forbes. It's Thursday, July 23. Today, we're highlighting the key stories covering Foreign Policy, National Security, and a closer look at Innovation.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nLeading off with, Trump gives saudis concessions in tentative nuclear deal—as family business is increasingly tied to kingdom. Sara Dorn has been tracking this development and reports that\n\nPresident Donald Trump is widely expected to announce an agreement allowing Saudi Arabia to enrich nuclear fuel, a deal critics warn includes concessions that could ease the kingdom's path to developing nuclear weapons. This controversial agreement comes as Trump and his family have significantly strengthened their business ties with Saudi Arabia during his second term. Their financial relationship with the Saudis dates back to the 1980s, but has intensified, with at least nine deals since his first term, reportedly worth $50 million in 2024 alone. Recent ventures include licensing the Trump name for a Maldives hotel and other projects in Jeddah and Riyadh with Saudi developer Dar Al Arkan. Despite these extensive dealings, Trump met with Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman in November, claiming his family had \"done very little\" business with Saudi Arabia. He has also repeatedly defended MBS regarding the murder of journalist Jamal Khashoggi, dismissing U.S. intelligence findings. During his first administration, an associate had previously suggested American companies help Saudi Arabia build nuclear power plants.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, Trump says U.S. will bomb key infrastructure ‘any time’ iran shoots at ships in strait of hormuz, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in National Security.\n\nPresident Donald Trump announced Wednesday that the U.S. will bomb a bridge or power plant, including those near Tehran, each time Iran attacks a ship in the Strait of Hormuz, escalating rhetoric amid a likely prolonged conflict. Trump's Truth Social post follows Secretary of State Marco Rubio's statement that the U.S. has pursued good-faith negotiations for 18 months but won't tolerate attacks, though talks remain possible if Iran is serious. This warning comes after 11 consecutive nights of U.S. strikes against Iranian military targets, as U.S. Central Command reported Iran has attacked over 30 commercial vessels in the Strait recently. Trump also reiterated threats to bomb Iran's Pickaxe Mountain, a suspected nuclear facility where Israel believes centrifuges have been moved, following the June 2025 bombing of Natanz. The war, fueled by disputes over Strait of Hormuz control, has seen a memorandum of understanding unravel due to Iran's refusal to allow free transit. The conflict is widening, with Iran-backed Houthi rebels blockading Saudi ships and threatening Red Sea vessels, prompting Trump's threats. The Pentagon seeks an additional $67 billion for the war, which has already cost $37.5 billion. Despite Trump's claims of public support, a Politico poll indicates declining MAGA support due to economic costs.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, America’s most innovative large companies: how we crunch the numbers. This headline is generating significant interest within Innovation.\n\nForbes is launching a new list, \"America's Most Innovative Large Companies,\" on August 25. Innovation is crucial for corporate survival. This data-driven list employs an objective Innovation Index to identify 100 top-performing firms from the S\u0026P 500. Key metrics include the \"Innovation Boost,\" assessing investor confidence via price-to-book value, adjusted for industry. An \"IP Activity Score\" from IPQwery measures intellectual property creation and defense. \"R\u0026D Focus\" tracks research and development spending as a sales percentage, weighted by company age. \"Return on Equity\" evaluates efficiency in generating value from shareholder equity. SignalAI further provides sentiment analysis from global media and social media regarding innovation. Kumkuat AI utilizes synthetic audiences to evaluate real-world product launches and events based on significance, commercial viability, and competitive difficulty. These diverse, normalized, and industry-adjusted scores are combined to rank the 100 most innovative companies.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"Trump Gives Saudis Concessions In Tentative Nuclear Deal—As Family Business Is Increasingly Tied To Kingdom","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/07/22/trump-gives-saudis-concessions-in-tentative-nuclear-deal-as-family-business-is-increasingly-tied-to-kingdom/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a6113a025d2c99a9e890fd8/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"President Donald Trump is widely expected to announce an agreement allowing Saudi Arabia to enrich nuclear fuel, a deal critics warn includes concessions that could ease the kingdom's path to developing nuclear weapons. This controversial agreement comes as Trump and his family have significantly strengthened their business ties with Saudi Arabia during his second term. Their financial relationship with the Saudis dates back to the 1980s, but has intensified, with at least nine deals since his first term, reportedly worth $50 million in 2024 alone. Recent ventures include licensing the Trump name for a Maldives hotel and other projects in Jeddah and Riyadh with Saudi developer Dar Al Arkan. Despite these extensive dealings, Trump met with Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman in November, claiming his family had \"done very little\" business with Saudi Arabia. He has also repeatedly defended MBS regarding the murder of journalist Jamal Khashoggi, dismissing U.S. intelligence findings. During his first administration, an associate had previously suggested American companies help Saudi Arabia build nuclear power plants.","shortHeadline":"Trump's Saudi Concessions","topic":"Foreign Policy","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a61136f79e033a5d630ce28"},{"title":"Trump Says U.S. Will Bomb Key Infrastructure ‘Any Time’ Iran Shoots At Ships In Strait Of Hormuz","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/07/22/trump-says-us-will-bomb-key-infrastructure-any-time-iran-shoots-at-ships-in-strait-of-hormuz/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a60d31d92a9e74afcb527c6/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"President Donald Trump announced Wednesday that the U.S. will bomb a bridge or power plant, including those near Tehran, each time Iran attacks a ship in the Strait of Hormuz, escalating rhetoric amid a likely prolonged conflict. Trump's Truth Social post follows Secretary of State Marco Rubio's statement that the U.S. has pursued good-faith negotiations for 18 months but won't tolerate attacks, though talks remain possible if Iran is serious. This warning comes after 11 consecutive nights of U.S. strikes against Iranian military targets, as U.S. Central Command reported Iran has attacked over 30 commercial vessels in the Strait recently. Trump also reiterated threats to bomb Iran's Pickaxe Mountain, a suspected nuclear facility where Israel believes centrifuges have been moved, following the June 2025 bombing of Natanz. The war, fueled by disputes over Strait of Hormuz control, has seen a memorandum of understanding unravel due to Iran's refusal to allow free transit. The conflict is widening, with Iran-backed Houthi rebels blockading Saudi ships and threatening Red Sea vessels, prompting Trump's threats. The Pentagon seeks an additional $67 billion for the war, which has already cost $37.5 billion. Despite Trump's claims of public support, a Politico poll indicates declining MAGA support due to economic costs.","shortHeadline":"Iran Infrastructure Threat","topic":"National Security","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a60d287bf985fd446342fa0"},{"title":"America’s Most Innovative Large Companies: How We Crunch The Numbers","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/hnewman/2026/07/22/americas-most-innovative-large-companies-how-we-crunch-the-numbers/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a610f643a13c3e94bcddb22/960x0.jpg","author":"Heather Newman","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/1752646","authorUrl":"https://www.forbes.com/sites/hnewman","authorBio":"Heather Newman is Deputy Editor, List and Data Strategy at Forbes. She has been an award-winning business, gaming and technology journalist for more than 25 years, including more than a decade at Forbes. She frequently writes business-related list stories and gaming interviews. She holds a master’s degree in computer science with a specialization in artificial intelligence, and has written multiple academic papers for publication. Her work has appeared in outlets including USA Today, Rolling Stone, Dateline NBC, NPR, PC Gamer, Polygon, PCGamesN, VentureBeat/GamesBeat, UploadVR and 75 newspapers in the U.S. and overseas. She’s also the author of three books on home computing topics for general audiences and young adults. Follow her on Twitter/X @gbitses.","authorAvatar":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/2785dfb8819549a5e582957753378d76?s=400\u0026d=mm\u0026r=g","authorSlug":"hnewman","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Forbes is launching a new list, \"America's Most Innovative Large Companies,\" on August 25. Innovation is crucial for corporate survival. This data-driven list employs an objective Innovation Index to identify 100 top-performing firms from the S\u0026P 500. Key metrics include the \"Innovation Boost,\" assessing investor confidence via price-to-book value, adjusted for industry. An \"IP Activity Score\" from IPQwery measures intellectual property creation and defense. \"R\u0026D Focus\" tracks research and development spending as a sales percentage, weighted by company age. \"Return on Equity\" evaluates efficiency in generating value from shareholder equity. SignalAI further provides sentiment analysis from global media and social media regarding innovation. Kumkuat AI utilizes synthetic audiences to evaluate real-world product launches and events based on significance, commercial viability, and competitive difficulty. These diverse, normalized, and industry-adjusted scores are combined to rank the 100 most innovative companies.","shortHeadline":"Company Innovation Rankings","topic":"Innovation","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/3809-6a60fb5c82ba9f51b2d87861"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/4G2nND91-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"4G2nND91","audioDurationS":294,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-07-23T06:30:40-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-22-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-22","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, the essential news digest from the Forbes newsroom. It's Wednesday, July 22. Today, we're exploring the stories shaping the global conversation—from the latest in Global Mobility and Aviation to a deep dive into Elections.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nWe begin with a look at, US passport stuck at no. 10 in power rankings—as 36 countries offer more freedom of movement. Suzanne Rowan Kelleher has been following this story and reports that\n\nThe U.S. passport's global power has significantly declined over the past two decades, falling from a tied No. 1 position in 2006 to tied No. 10 today on the Henley Passport Index. Americans currently enjoy visa-free travel to 180 countries, placing them behind 36 other nations offering greater mobility. This drop is partly attributed to the U.S.'s lack of reciprocity, as it only allows 46 nationalities visa-free entry, ranking 73rd on the Henley Openness Index. Consequently, U.S. citizens still require visas for countries like China. The diminishing power of the U.S. passport, coupled with geopolitical uncertainties, is driving a surge in affluent Americans seeking second or third citizenships. Experts suggest that true wealth is increasingly defined by the freedom to choose where one can live, work, and invest globally.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou can find the full analysis of that story at Forbes.com. Now, we're turning our attention to, 6,500 flights disrupted from east coast thunderstorms, a development that could have a lasting impact on Aviation.\n\nSevere weather is causing widespread air travel chaos across the U.S. on Tuesday, with over 6,500 flights disrupted, including more than 1,900 cancellations and 4,600 delays by Tuesday afternoon. East Coast hubs are particularly affected, experiencing ground stops at major airports like Boston Logan, Newark Liberty, Reagan National, and Baltimore/Washington. John F. Kennedy, LaGuardia, and Newark airports reported triple-digit flight cancellations. The disruptions stem from expected thunderstorms and flash flooding stretching from the Mid-South to the Northeast, bringing severe wind gusts, flash flooding, and potential hail and tornadoes. In response, major carriers like American, Delta, United, Southwest, and JetBlue have issued travel waivers, enabling passengers to rebook flights without incurring additional fees for travel to or from affected airports.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRounding out today's briefing, we look at, Hundreds of noncitizens voted in new jersey after DMV mistakenly registered them. This story is currently at the center of the conversation in Elections.\n\nNew Jersey's Department of Motor Vehicles improperly registered approximately 6,600 noncitizens to vote due to a software error, with about 400 casting ballots between June 2023 and June 2024. Governor Mikie Sherrill confirmed the error, explaining individuals answered \"no\" to citizenship but were registered anyway. Sherrill announced all affected noncitizens would be removed from voter rolls and the software vendor replaced. This incident is expected to fuel election fraud claims from former President Donald Trump and his allies, who advocate for stricter voter ID laws. The White House and Republicans are promoting the SAVE Act, which would mandate states submit voter rolls for federal review, arguing it would prevent such issues. Critics warn the SAVE Act could grant the government unrestricted access to sensitive data. Governor Sherrill, a Democrat, criticized Trump's \"zero credibility\" on election integrity, citing his history of undermining elections. Despite this New Jersey anomaly, investigations consistently show noncitizen voting is rare, with a federal tool reporting only 0.04% of verification cases as noncitizens, and a conservative database finding just 77 such cases between 1999 and 2023. Trump continues to push for the SAVE Act, facing Democratic opposition in the Senate, while House Republicans seek to include elements in a defense spending bill.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou've reached the end of this Daily Brief. Tune in again for tomorrow's updates. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":0,"articles":[{"title":"US Passport Stuck At No. 10 In Power Rankings—As 36 Countries Offer More Freedom Of Movement","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/2026/07/21/us-passport-no-10-power-rankings/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/651d3b59e94c11b342309534/960x0.jpg","author":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3340630","authorUrl":"h