nding bill.","shortHeadline":"Noncitizen Voting","topic":"Elections","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a5fc057577595ed36c3ed53"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/PUAroewT-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"PUAroewT","audioDurationS":269,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-07-22T06:31:01-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-21-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-21","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Tuesday, July 21. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Politics, Defense, and Business Deals.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Politics space, we start with, Darline graham’s political views largely unknown as she launches senate campaign. According to reporting from Sara Dorn\n\nSen. Darline Graham Nordone, R-S.C., the younger sister of the late Sen. Lindsey Graham, announced her bid to permanently succeed her brother, whom she was appointed to replace temporarily. Nordone, who served as commissioner for the South Carolina Commission for the Blind since 2019, has a background in disability rights advocacy, including helping to end subminimum wage policy in the state. Colleagues describe her as not overtly political, with her only recorded political donation being $100. She was raised by her brother after their parents' deaths when she was 13 and often campaigned with him. Nordone expressed confidence in her ability to serve, stating she learned hard work from Lindsey. Her candidacy comes with an endorsement from former President Donald Trump, who called her a \"spectacular person.\" She will compete in a special primary on August 11 against Republican Reps. Russell Fry and Ralph Norman, among others, with the winner facing Democrat Annie Andrews in November. Lindsey Graham, a four-term senator and foreign policy hawk, died on July 11.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, Pentagon denies hiding injury stats—after report dozens were hurt in undisclosed iran strikes, a story that could have significant implications for Defense.\n\nThe Pentagon on Monday denied allegations of concealing complete statistics regarding U.S. service members injured in the Iran war. This denial followed a New York Times report, citing unnamed U.S. officials, which claimed dozens of American troops were hurt in three previously undisclosed Iranian strikes last week. Department of Defense spokesperson Sean Parnell vehemently rejected these \"baseless and malicious accusations,\" suggesting the Times aimed to \"further distress the American people\" in the wake of recent fatalities. Parnell, however, did not directly address the three specific attacks mentioned by the Times. An unnamed U.S. military official reportedly told the Times that the Pentagon isn't obligated to release injury data, particularly when service members quickly return to duty, and has ceased daily announcements of strike locations to avoid providing intelligence to Iran. Parnell emphasized that U.S. military casualty information is publicly accessible and regularly updated online. This controversy arises as three U.S. service members were recently killed in Iranian attacks in Jordan and Iraq, bringing the total to 17, amidst an escalation of fighting following a collapsed ceasefire. Both nations have expressed openness to negotiations despite the ongoing conflict.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, How trump could profit from fast-tracked rebrand of palm beach’s airport. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Business Deals.\n\nPalm Beach International Airport has officially been renamed President Donald J. Trump International Airport, fulfilling a long-held desire for Trump, though the unusual licensing deal raises questions about future profits. The renaming cost is estimated at $5.5 million, with the state providing only half, leaving Palm Beach County to cover the shortfall. This marks the first time a sitting president has an airport named after them, a process that unfolded with unusual speed.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"Darline Graham’s Political Views Largely Unknown As She Launches Senate Campaign","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/07/20/darline-grahams-political-views-largely-unknown-as-she-launches-senate-campaign/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a5e9e3e7c769d99ff3add62/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donald Trump's federal election interference and classified documents cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Sen. Darline Graham Nordone, R-S.C., the younger sister of the late Sen. Lindsey Graham, announced her bid to permanently succeed her bro