cases and his Manhattan hush money case. Dorn graduated in 2012 from the University of Dayton with a degree in journalism. Prior to joining Forbes, she covered New York City and state politics for the New York Post and City and State magazine. Follow her for updates and analysis on the 2024 presidential race, key Senate and House races and developments in Congress and at the White House.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/63457b718c2404f82daa3149/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1284\u0026cropY1=403\u0026cropY2=1687","authorSlug":"saradorn","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"The Pentagon on Monday denied allegations of concealing complete statistics regarding U.S. service members injured in the Iran war. This denial followed a New York Times report, citing unnamed U.S. officials, which claimed dozens of American troops were hurt in three previously undisclosed Iranian strikes last week. Department of Defense spokesperson Sean Parnell vehemently rejected these \"baseless and malicious accusations,\" suggesting the Times aimed to \"further distress the American people\" in the wake of recent fatalities. Parnell, however, did not directly address the three specific attacks mentioned by the Times. An unnamed U.S. military official reportedly told the Times that the Pentagon isn't obligated to release injury data, particularly when service members quickly return to duty, and has ceased daily announcements of strike locations to avoid providing intelligence to Iran. Parnell emphasized that U.S. military casualty information is publicly accessible and regularly updated online. This controversy arises as three U.S. service members were recently killed in Iranian attacks in Jordan and Iraq, bringing the total to 17, amidst an escalation of fighting following a collapsed ceasefire. Both nations have expressed openness to negotiations despite the ongoing conflict.","shortHeadline":"Military Injury Denials","topic":"Defense","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51554-6a5e350b1429e9feb90d831e"},{"title":"How Trump Could Profit From Fast-Tracked Rebrand Of Palm Beach’s Airport","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/2026/07/20/trump-profit-rebrand-palm-beach-airport/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a5e2fc8e7144c34f9bfe4ba/960x0.jpg","author":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3340630","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/suzannerowankelleher/","authorBio":"Suzanne Rowan Kelleher covers travel for Forbes. Follow her on Bluesky @srkelleher.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/601b7235b35a3ce5939c28fe/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1080\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=1080","authorSlug":"suzannerowankelleher","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Palm Beach International Airport has officially been renamed President Donald J. Trump International Airport, fulfilling a long-held desire for Trump, though the unusual licensing deal raises questions about future profits. The renaming cost is estimated at $5.5 million, with the state providing only half, leaving Palm Beach County to cover the shortfall. This marks the first time a sitting president has an airport named after them, a process that unfolded with unusual speed.","shortHeadline":"Trump Airport Profits","topic":"Business Deals","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/7082-6a5e2f90d52c2c36594564ec"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/6C1coYuA-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"6C1coYuA","audioDurationS":245,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-07-21T06:30:43-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-20-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-20","script":"You're listening to The Daily Brief, your essential news update from Forbes. It's Monday, July 20. Today, we're highlighting the key stories covering Military Aviation, National Security, and a closer look at Hospitality.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nLeading off with, Blue angels pilot ‘shaken’ by ‘miscalculated turn’ in pensacola flyover MAGA celebrated, lawmaker says. Sara Dorn has been tracking this development and reports that\n\nA Blue Angels pilot expressed deep regret and felt \"shaken\" by his recent low-altitude flyover above a Pensacola, Florida, beach, which caused umbrellas and chairs to be blown over. Florida state Rep. Michelle Salzman conveyed the pilot's remorse, stating he admitted a \"miscalculated turn\" and was deeply affected by falling short of his high standards. This admission comes despite strong defenses from MAGA Republicans and Trump officials, who cheered the incident as a display of military pride and attacked critics. Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth and Navy Secretary Hung Cao quickly dismissed any need for reprimands, with Hegseth famously declaring, \"The flyovers will continue until morale improves.\" Eric Trump also blamed the media for \"manufactured outrage.\" The Navy initially stated it was reviewing the maneuver for safety standards. This incident follows a pattern of controversial low-altitude military flyovers, including Apache helicopters over South Carolina and a helicopter near Kid Rock's home, all of which Hegseth has defended, often linking them to a broader push for military masculinity.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, U.S. service member killed in iraq—after 2 deaths in jordan, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in National Security.\n\nA U.S. service member died in northern Iraq Saturday during an Iranian drone detonation, with another wounded. This occurred a day after an Iranian attack in Jordan killed two U.S. service members, with one still missing. These incidents bring total U.S. service member deaths in the Iran conflict to 17. The U.S. military completed an eighth consecutive night of strikes on Iran, targeting military coastal surveillance, air defense, maritime capabilities, and missile/drone storage sites. Specific strikes also hit IRGC forces responsible for the Jordan attack. Amid escalating tensions, the U.S. is deploying additional warplanes, including F-35s and F-16s, to the Middle East, though these deployments were pre-planned. The recent escalation between Iran and the U.S. follows the unraveling of a previous agreement to end the war, rooted in a Strait of Hormuz dispute. Former President Trump defended the conflict, asserting the Jordan deaths aimed to prevent Iran from acquiring nuclear weapons.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, IHG’s fastest-growth premium brand ‘voco’ is good news for loyalty program members. This headline is generating significant interest within Hospitality.\n\nIHG Hotels \u0026 Resorts' voco brand has rapidly expanded, reaching 100 hotels in seven years and now boasting 250 open or in-pipeline properties, targeting 200 within its first decade. As a soft brand, voco accelerates growth through conversions of existing hotels, a cost-effective strategy favored in today's high-interest, high-cost environment. This model allows hotel owners to access IHG's global distribution and loyalty programs while retaining their unique identity. For consumers, this means more diverse and characterful hotels are available for loyalty point earning and redemption. voco is expanding beyond urban areas into resorts, exemplified by its first international resort in Quang Binh, Vietnam, which is boosting local tourism and economy. This growth reflects a broader industry trend where soft brands are proving popular for investors and hotel owners, offering independent hotels the commercial benefits of a major chain without sacrificing their individuality. IHG continues to prioritize premium growth, with voco playing a key role in this strategy.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemp