ne and loyalty program industry from every corner of the globe. He explores the latest trends in travel to bring detailed insight to readers that help inform their own travel decisions. Ramsey regularly interviews influential business executives who shape how we see and experience the world. He flies more than 400,000 miles per year and has a passion for aviation (he even had childhood birthday parties at the airport). Ramsey has traveled to all 7 continents, 50 U.S. states and 175 United Nations countries. His stories have appeared in well-known publications including Conde Nast Traveler, Travel+Leisure, CNBC, NerdWallet, AFAR, Robb Report, Readers Digest, BBC Worldwide, USA Today, Bloomberg and Travel Weekly among others. Follow his work to gain insight on the latest news and trends in travel.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/5ec40fdfac01e2000762cf40/400x0.jpg?cropX1=28\u0026cropX2=335\u0026cropY1=77\u0026cropY2=384","authorSlug":"ramseyqubein","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"IHG Hotels \u0026 Resorts' voco brand has rapidly expanded, reaching 100 hotels in seven years and now boasting 250 open or in-pipeline properties, targeting 200 within its first decade. As a soft brand, voco accelerates growth through conversions of existing hotels, a cost-effective strategy favored in today's high-interest, high-cost environment. This model allows hotel owners to access IHG's global distribution and loyalty programs while retaining their unique identity. For consumers, this means more diverse and characterful hotels are available for loyalty point earning and redemption. voco is expanding beyond urban areas into resorts, exemplified by its first international resort in Quang Binh, Vietnam, which is boosting local tourism and economy. This growth reflects a broader industry trend where soft brands are proving popular for investors and hotel owners, offering independent hotels the commercial benefits of a major chain without sacrificing their individuality. IHG continues to prioritize premium growth, with voco playing a key role in this strategy.","shortHeadline":"Hotel Loyalty Programs","topic":"Hospitality","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/50404-6a5b4892873f7e14d725c009"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/z7LJze2K-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"z7LJze2K","audioDurationS":257,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-07-20T06:30:46-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-19-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-19","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, the essential news digest from the Forbes newsroom. It's Sunday, July 19. Today, we're exploring the stories shaping the global conversation—from the latest in Travel and Technology to a deep dive into Travel.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nWe begin with a look at, Why cruising from copenhagen works so well. David Nikel has been following this story and reports that\n\nCopenhagen has firmly established itself as a leading cruise hub in Northern Europe, projecting 880,000 passengers in 2026 and a 10% rise in turnaround calls to 122. Geographically, Copenhagen provides an ideal starting point for Baltic Sea itineraries, allowing for port-intensive voyages without extensive sea days to reach the region. It also serves as an excellent gateway for Norwegian fjords cruises, offering a contrasting Scandinavian experience. Beyond its logistical advantages, Copenhagen itself is a vibrant destination, encouraging passengers to extend their trips and explore its rich cultural offerings. This blend of efficient transport links, strategic location, modern facilities, and an appealing city makes Copenhagen an unusually strong cruise embarkation point.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou can find the full analysis of that story at Forbes.com. Now, we're turning our attention to, Xpeng expands beyond evs with munich event, promising flying cars and humanoid robots, a development that could have a lasting impact on Technology.\n\nChinese EV maker Xpeng launched its new L03 electric SUV in Munich, presenting an impressively-spec'd vehicle that undercuts competitors like Tesla and Hyundai on price, starting at €35,600 with a 445km WLTP range. However, Xpeng used the event to declare its evolution beyond a mere carmaker, positioning itself as a \"technology company that makes cars.\" The company introduced its \"Physical AI\" vision, integrating AI powered by self-developed chips and large language models with physical systems, hardware, and operating systems. This foundational technology underpins three ambitious applications: electric vehicles, humanoid robots, and \"flying cars.\" Despite these seeming futuristic, Xpeng founder He Xiaopeng asserted that both their humanoid robot, Iron, and the modular \"land aircraft carrier\" flying car, Aridge – which has been in development for over 13 years – are nearing mass production. He even promised their availability in Europe in the foreseeable future. Xpeng has already demonstrated significant success in the EV market, having shipped over a million vehicles in China and more than 60,000 in Europe since 2024, operating in 65 countries globally. Further solidifying its European presence, Xpeng announced a partnership with Google Maps, making it the first Asian EV manufacturer to offer fully integrated Google Maps navigation outside China, complementing its dedicated R\u0026D center in Munich.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRounding out today's briefing, we look at, Why antiparos is greece’s most discreet island escape. This story is currently at the center of the conversation in Travel.\n\nAntiparos, once a quiet neighbor to Paros, has transformed into a sophisticated Cycladic destination, drawing celebrities like Tom Hanks and affluent visitors while remarkably preserving its authentic, low-key charm. The island successfully balances exclusivity with an unpretentious atmosphere, avoiding the overt commercialism of other hotspots. Its main town, Chora, offers charming pedestrian streets, cafes, and boutiques leading to the Venetian Kastro, while outside, serene beaches and villas abound. Upscale accommodations include The Rooster, a 17-bungalow property known for its secluded, natural design, farm-to-table dining, and comprehensive wellness programs featuring Ayurveda. The more casual Beach House provides a direct beachfront experience. Antiparos boasts a diverse culinary scene, from traditional tavernas like Captain Pipinos to modern Italian at Salerosso and The Rooster's refined Secret Garden. Shopping in Chora features curated fashion and artisan goods. Visitors can explore the dramatic Cave of Antiparos, embark on boat trips to the archaeological site of Despotiko, discover hidden sea caves, or relax on various picturesque beaches.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou've reached the end of this Daily Brief. Tune in again for tomorrow's updates. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":0,"articles":[{"title":"Why Cruising From Copenhagen Works So Well","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/davidnikel/2026/07/18/why-cruising-from-copenhagen-works-so-well/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a5b7f431b408ea2d33dd587/960x0.jpg","author":"David Nikel","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/3339225","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/davidnikel/","authorBio":"David Nikel is a British-Norwegian travel writer based in Norway, covering all things travel with a special focus on cruising and Scandinavia. A frequent cruiser, Nikel shares developments and trends from the global cruising industry. As a published guidebook author and owner of the website Life in Norway, he also shares travel stories from Scandinavia and the Nordic region. Follow Nikel to help get the most out of your next cruise vacation or trip to Northern Europe.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/5f46708af8880e38567fa9b8/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=224\u0026cropY1=0\u0026cropY2=224","authorSlug":"davidnikel","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Copenhagen has firmly established itself as a leading cruise hub in Northern Europe, projecting 880,000 passengers in 2026 and a 10% rise in turnaround calls to 122. Geographically, Copenhagen provides an ideal starting point for Baltic Sea itineraries, allowing for port-intensive voyages without extensive sea days to reach the region. It also serves as an excellent gateway for Norwegian fjords cruises, offering a contrasting Scandinavian experience. Beyond its logistical advantages, Copenhagen itself is a vibrant destination, encouraging passengers to extend their trips and explore its rich cultural offerings. This blend of efficient transport links, strategic location, modern facilities, and an appealing city makes Copenhagen an unusually strong cruise embarkation point.","shortHeadline":"Copenhagen Cruises","topic":"Travel","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/6560-6a59e2240a7ec96e17cf1db0"},{"title":"Xpeng Expands Beyond EVs With Munich Event, Promising Flying Cars and Humanoid Robots","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/bensin/2026/07/18/xpeng-expands-beyond-evs-with-munich-event-promising-flying-cars-and-humanoid-robots/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a594f3731a74cd93029ec8f/960x0.jpg","author":"Ben Sin","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/325280","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/bensin/","authorBio":"I'm a Chinese-American journalist in Hong Kong, covering consumer tech in Asia. Before focusing on this exciting beat, I was a general culture writer and editor with bylines in the New York Times, Wall Street Journal, Sports Illustrated, New York Magazine, among others. \n\nFeel free to email me at bencsin@gmail.com","authorAvatar":"https://blogs-images.forbes.com/bensin/files/2018/05/Ben-Sin_avatar_1525189111.png","authorSlug":"bensin","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Chinese EV maker Xpeng launched its new L03 electric SUV in Munich, presenting 