nd professional raconteur, specializing in exclusive travel, extreme luxury, and high-end destinations.For over 12 years, he has been writing for Forbes, focusing on rare access to extraordinary people, places, and things such as superyachts, underground bunkers, and luxury real estate.An American based in Italy, Dobson has spent over 45 years traveling and has visited more than 90 countries. His work emphasizes immersive, firsthand experiences in remote destinations, often highlighting places that are largely inaccessible to the public.Before focusing on journalism, Dobson built a career in the entertainment industry, working with prominent celebrities and billionaires. This experience informs his access-driven approach and narrative style.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve//62bf82cb95d8841bbb8bca9b/400x0.jpg?cropX1=68\u0026cropX2=1217\u0026cropY1=152\u0026cropY2=1301","authorSlug":"jimdobson","authorType":"Contributor","summary":"Antiparos, once a quiet neighbor to Paros, has transformed into a sophisticated Cycladic destination, drawing celebrities like Tom Hanks and affluent visitors while remarkably preserving its authentic, low-key charm. The island successfully balances exclusivity with an unpretentious atmosphere, avoiding the overt commercialism of other hotspots. Its main town, Chora, offers charming pedestrian streets, cafes, and boutiques leading to the Venetian Kastro, while outside, serene beaches and villas abound. Upscale accommodations include The Rooster, a 17-bungalow property known for its secluded, natural design, farm-to-table dining, and comprehensive wellness programs featuring Ayurveda. The more casual Beach House provides a direct beachfront experience. Antiparos boasts a diverse culinary scene, from traditional tavernas like Captain Pipinos to modern Italian at Salerosso and The Rooster's refined Secret Garden. Shopping in Chora features curated fashion and artisan goods. Visitors can explore the dramatic Cave of Antiparos, embark on boat trips to the archaeological site of Despotiko, discover hidden sea caves, or relax on various picturesque beaches.","shortHeadline":"Greek Island Escapes","topic":"Travel","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/3307-6a1e9d37d1638f3d9c3720f1"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/zRyIuVBS-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"zRyIuVBS","audioDurationS":282,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-07-19T08:30:41-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-18-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-18","script":"Welcome to The Daily Brief, a look at today's top reporting from Forbes. It's Saturday, July 18. On the agenda today: the latest developments in Trade, Travel Tech, and Medical Safety.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nDiving into the Trade space, we start with, Trump threatens canada with new tariffs for wildfires. According to reporting from Sara Dorn\n\nFormer President Donald Trump announced plans to increase tariffs on Canada, citing poor U.S. air quality from Canadian wildfire smoke. In a Truth Social post, Trump accused Canada of failing to properly maintain its forests, leading to \"filthy, polluted, and unhealthy air\" invading the United States. He insisted the cost of this pollution must be added to Canada's existing tariffs. However, the mechanism or authority for Trump to tax Canada for pollution remains unclear, as tariffs typically apply to physical goods. Trump also stated he would contact Canadian Prime Minister Mark Carney to address the wildfire situation and air quality concerns. This comes as 122 Canadian wildfires were \"out of control\" as of Friday, causing widespread health alerts across the northeastern U.S. and Great Lakes. Separately, FIFA president Gianni Infantino was expected to meet Trump to discuss potential health hazards for the upcoming World Cup final in New Jersey due to the smoke. Other Republican lawmakers, including Sen. Bernie Moreno, have also threatened to sanction Canada, criticizing its perceived lack of investment in wildfire prevention.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nFor more context on that story, visit Forbes.com. Next, we shift our focus to, Can $1 billion fora travel challenge expedia and booking.com?, a story that could have significant implications for Travel Tech.\n\nFora Travel has reached unicorn status, raising $60 million in Series D funding at a $1 billion valuation, bringing total investment to $138.5 million. This significant capital underscores investor confidence in Fora's rapidly growing network of over 15,000 travel advisors and its AI-powered tech. Since 2021, Fora's advisors have booked over $3 billion in travel, demonstrating impressive acceleration. Investors see Fora transforming the labor-intensive travel sector into a scalable tech platform and dominant distribution channel. The company's model blends human expertise for complex, high-value luxury travel with AI for efficiency, offering both speed and trusted curation, unlike traditional online agencies. New funds will enhance its AI assistant, Via, and expand into flights, cruises, and new markets. This strategy capitalizes on the multi-trillion-dollar travel industry's modernization needs and the resurgence of travel advisors, even among younger demographics. Fora's success validates a strategic bet on human-centric travel, augmented by technology, to capture a larger market share.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nOur final story today looks at, 3 initiatives that made this top hospital safer. This remains a major point of conversation in the world of Medical Safety.\n\nForbes has unveiled its \"Top Hospitals By State 2026\" list, honoring 781 facilities for their exceptional commitment to patient care. This recognition stems from a rigorous methodology, drawing from the Forbes Top Hospitals 2026 national list and utilizing vetted Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) data to identify the top 30% of general acute care hospitals in each state. These rankings underscore a dedication to preventing harms like post-surgical complications, medication errors, and readmissions. St. Luke’s Magic Valley Medical Center, a 5-star rated institution, exemplifies this commitment by fostering a culture where staff are empowered to proactively improve care. Key initiatives at St. Luke's include universal nasal decolonization before surgery, which dramatically reduced surgical site infections by targeting patient-borne bacteria. They also successfully eliminated catheter-associated urinary tract infections (CAUTIs) since September 2024 by re-evaluating catheter use and reinforcing protocols. Furthermore, St. Luke's addressed hospital readmissions through enhanced discharge education, including sepsis-specific information packs, and a free paramedic follow-up program for high-risk patients. These multi-pronged efforts, crucially involving frontline staff, ensure smoother patient transitions and underscore the importance of making quality care both effective and accessible.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nYou're all caught up with The Daily Brief. Join us again tomorrow for your next news digest. Thanks for joining us.","scriptTemplateId":2,"articles":[{"title":"Trump Threatens Canada With New Tariffs For Wildfires","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn/2026/07/17/trump-threatens-canada-with-new-tariffs-for-wildfires/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a5a8c85cab56d7ae93824f2/960x0.jpg","author":"Sara Dorn","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/5010031","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/saradorn","authorBio":"Sara Dorn is a Forbes news reporter who covers politics, with a focus on elections and Capitol Hill. She joined Forbes in 2022 and is based in New York. She's covered the 2022 midterms, 2024 presidential campaign, the January 6 House committee investigation, former House Speaker Kevin McCarthy's historic ouster, the 2023 State of the Union Address, former President Donal