el Pain: From Everyday Aches to Chronic Conditions, which won the 2023 Science in Society Journalism Book Award from the National Association of Science Writers. Previously, she was on staff at several publications including Consumer Reports where her investigations into dangerous infant sleepers and unstable dressers spurred the recall of millions of products and led to new laws. For this work, she won the Deadline Club Award for Magazine Investigative Reporting from the Society of Professional Journalists and was a finalist for the National Magazine Award for Public Service from the American Society of Magazine Editors. Peachman has a B.A. from the University of Michigan and an M.S. from Columbia Journalism School. Follow her on Twitter @RachelPeachman, email her at rpeachman [at] forbes.com or find her at: https://www.rachelrabkinpeachman.com/Forbes reporters follow company ethical guidelines that ensure the highest quality.","authorAvatar":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/671281d4377336b60e01a30a/400x0.jpg?cropX1=0\u0026cropX2=1207\u0026cropY1=91\u0026cropY2=1299","authorSlug":"rachelpeachman","authorType":"Forbes Staff","summary":"Forbes has unveiled its \"Top Hospitals By State 2026\" list, honoring 781 facilities for their exceptional commitment to patient care. This recognition stems from a rigorous methodology, drawing from the Forbes Top Hospitals 2026 national list and utilizing vetted Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) data to identify the top 30% of general acute care hospitals in each state. These rankings underscore a dedication to preventing harms like post-surgical complications, medication errors, and readmissions. St. Luke’s Magic Valley Medical Center, a 5-star rated institution, exemplifies this commitment by fostering a culture where staff are empowered to proactively improve care. Key initiatives at St. Luke's include universal nasal decolonization before surgery, which dramatically reduced surgical site infections by targeting patient-borne bacteria. They also successfully eliminated catheter-associated urinary tract infections (CAUTIs) since September 2024 by re-evaluating catheter use and reinforcing protocols. Furthermore, St. Luke's addressed hospital readmissions through enhanced discharge education, including sepsis-specific information packs, and a free paramedic follow-up program for high-risk patients. These multi-pronged efforts, crucially involving frontline staff, ensure smoother patient transitions and underscore the importance of making quality care both effective and accessible.","shortHeadline":"Hospital Safety Initiatives","topic":"Medical Safety","naturalId":"blogAndPostId/blog/post/51648-6a5512e7d75de53de400bee2"}],"audioUrl":"https://cdn.jwplayer.com/videos/KfHuHFMf-3GQxW5ax.m4a","mediaId":"KfHuHFMf","audioDurationS":299,"audioFormat":"audio/mp4","generatedAt":"2026-07-18T08:30:42-05:00"},{"id":"BRIEF-2026-07-17-MORNING","cadence":"morning","date":"2026-07-17","script":"You're listening to The Daily Brief, your essential news update from Forbes. It's Friday, July 17. Today, we're highlighting the key stories covering Consumer Trends, Military, and a closer look at Politics.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nLeading off with, Can’t get a piece of openai or anthropic? there’s a booming secondary market for their swag. Anna Tong has been tracking this development and reports that\n\nUnable to invest in upcoming AI company IPOs, enthusiasts are fueling a booming secondary market for AI company swag on eBay. Items like OpenAI hoodies and Anthropic pens are reselling for hundreds of dollars, significantly surpassing the value of merchandise from other tech giants. This \"geek chic\" trend offers a sense of belonging and \"Silicon Valley street cred\" to buyers, many residing outside major tech hubs. Sellers confirm that hyper-limited, employee-issued items tied to product launches are particularly coveted, with buyers meticulously verifying authenticity. OpenAI has capitalized on this hype by launching \"OpenAI Supply Co.\" to the public, selling custom garments and framing them as \"physical expressions of research energy.\" This phenomenon echoes past \"swag bubbles,\" such as Silicon Valley Bank's, though that one quickly deflated. Historically, tech memorabilia, especially Apple artifacts, has become a multi-million-dollar asset class, with some items selling for millions. Experts predict AI swag could mature into a formal auction category, driven by the desire for \"lore\" and \"bragging rights\" among collectors.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nRead the full report on that story at Forbes.com. Moving now to, ‘No reprimands’ for blue angels flyover that ripped past pensacola beach, navy secretary says, a story that's currently shaping the landscape in Military.\n\nDefense Secretary Pete Hegseth announced that military pilots will continue conducting low-altitude flights for entertainment purposes \"until morale improves,\" following a debate over disciplining pilots for low flyovers on the South Carolina coast over July Fourth. Hegseth reversed the suspensions of eight Apache pilots involved in the Independence Day incident, vowing to \"fix\" the situation and posting \"Carry on, Patriots\" on X. This action mirrors his intervention in March, when he reversed suspensions for two Apache pilots who flew near musician Kid Rock’s Nashville home. The military has faced significant recruitment, retention, and morale challenges since the COVID-19 pandemic, exacerbated by the Iran war, despite recent improvements in recruitment figures. While the South Carolina National Guard initially described the suspensions as a \"routine administrative measure,\" Hegseth's decision, influenced by backlash from Republican lawmakers including Gov. Henry McMaster, ensures there will be \"no punishment\" and \"no investigation\" for the pilots. This policy aims to bolster military spirits amidst ongoing difficulties.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nTo finish today's update, we're turning to, Reid hoffman gives $10 million in support of texas senate candidate james talarico. This headline is generating significant interest within Politics.\n\nDemocratic megadonor Reid Hoffman, co-founder of LinkedIn, has injected $10 million into James Talarico’s Texas Senate campaign, marking the largest super PAC donation in the second quarter. This substantial contribution supports Talarico’s pivotal bid against Republican Ken Paxton, a race crucial for Democrats hoping to gain control of the Senate. Hoffman, a prolific Democratic donor with an estimated $2.7 billion net worth, has given approximately $77 million to Democratic causes since 2016. Talarico’s campaign amassed $30 million in Q2 donations, significantly outraising Paxton’s $9 million. The contest remains incredibly tight, with a June poll showing both candidates at 47% support. Paxton previously unseated incumbent Senator John Cornyn in the primary runoff, aided by former President Trump's endorsement. However, both candidates face scrutiny. Talarico has drawn criticism for past remarks, including describing himself as a \"Christian who hates Christianity\" and claiming six biological sexes. This high-stakes election highlights the intense financial and political battle for the Texas Senate seat.\n\n\u003cbreak time=\"1.12s\"/\u003e\n\nThat wraps up this edition of The Daily Brief. Be sure to check back tomorrow for our next update. Thanks for listening.","scriptTemplateId":1,"articles":[{"title":"Can’t Get A Piece Of OpenAI Or Anthropic? There’s A Booming Secondary Market  For Their Swag","url":"http://www.forbes.com/sites/annatong/2026/07/16/cant-get-a-piece-of-openai-or-anthropic-theres-a-booming-secondary-market--for-their-swag/","image":"https://specials-images.forbesimg.com/imageserve/6a57cab83c7be79e17768a11/960x0.jpg","author":"Anna Tong","authorNaturalId":"blogAuthorId/blog/author/7244464","authorUrl":"http://www.forbes.c